/* Question bank: 2026 assignments (official solutions), 2019 assignments (accepted answers), NPTEL AI mock papers 1-5 */
window.QBANK=[{"id":"A26-1-1","src":"A26","wk":1,"n":1,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following can be considered a job (routine operation) rather than a project?","opts":["Processing monthly employee salaries","Developing a new mobile application","Manufacturing cars on an assembly line","Organizing the Olympic Games","Handling daily customer support requests"],"ans":[0,2,4],"exp":"Processing monthly salaries, manufacturing cars on an assembly line, and handling daily customer support requests are all repetitive, ongoing operations with well-defined procedures. They lack the temporary, unique, and goal-oriented nature of projects, which have a clear start and end, such as developing a new app or organizing the Olympics.","topic":"t-intro"},{"id":"A26-1-2","src":"A26","wk":1,"n":2,"type":"msq","text":"Software projects are generally considered harder to manage than manufacturing projects. Which of the following are possible reasons for this?","opts":["Software products are intangible","Software development involves highly skilled intellectual work","Software projects are always larger than manufacturing projects","Requirement changes can have significant effects on software projects","Software projects require people management"],"ans":[0,1,3],"exp":"Software products are intangible, making progress difficult to measure visually, and development relies on highly skilled intellectual work, which is less predictable than manufacturing tasks. Additionally, requirement changes can have a significant ripple effect on software projects due to their malleable nature, whereas manufacturing changes are often costlier to implement later. People management, while important, is required in all projects and is not a unique reason for software's added difficulty.","topic":"t-intro"},{"id":"A26-1-3","src":"A26","wk":1,"n":3,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements about software complexity are true?","opts":["Professional developers usually make any software that they develop as complex as possible","Making changes to software for maintenance purposes does not affect its complexity","Increased software complexity generally makes maintenance more difficult","Repeated modifications can increase software complexity","Software complexity automatically decreases as software ages","Higher software complexity can increase the likelihood of introducing defects during maintenance"],"ans":[2,3,5],"exp":"Increased software complexity makes the code harder to understand and modify, directly increasing maintenance difficulty. Repeated modifications over time tend to add structural clutter (often called software entropy), which raises complexity, and this higher complexity increases the chance of introducing new defects when changes are made. Professional developers aim for simplicity, maintenance changes do affect complexity, and complexity does not automatically decrease with age—it typically grows unless actively refactored.","topic":"t-intro"},{"id":"A26-1-4","src":"A26","wk":1,"n":4,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements regarding project stakeholders are true?","opts":["Sponsors are not considered stakeholders because they only provide resources for the project","A stakeholder can be an individual or an organization","Stakeholders may influence project execution and outcomes","Customers are stakeholders because they use the project's product or service","Only project team members are the stakeholders of the project"],"ans":[1,2,3],"exp":"Stakeholders include any individual or organization that is actively involved in or affected by the project, which covers sponsors, customers, users, and the project team. They can influence project execution and outcomes, and customers are indeed stakeholders because they use or are impacted by the final product. Sponsors are also key stakeholders despite providing resources, and limiting stakeholders to just the project team is incorrect.","topic":"t-intro"},{"id":"A26-1-5","src":"A26","wk":1,"n":5,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following factors can negatively affect the success of a software project?","opts":["Frequent changes in business needs","Inexperienced project team members","Effective project management","Rapid changes in the chosen technology","Poor understanding of customer expectations"],"ans":[0,1,3,4],"exp":"Frequent changes in business needs, inexperienced team members, rapid technology shifts, and poor understanding of customer expectations all introduce uncertainty, rework, and misalignment, which directly harm project success. In contrast, effective project management is a positive factor that helps mitigate risks and keep the project on track.","topic":"t-intro"},{"id":"A26-1-6","src":"A26","wk":1,"n":6,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements is correct regarding various types of software projects?","opts":["Packaged software is rarely available for the vertical market","Packaged software is intended for multiple customers","Custom software is always developed from scratch","Custom software is developed to satisfy specific user requirements","Packaged software is always cheaper than custom software"],"ans":[1,3],"exp":"Packaged software is built for multiple customers with common needs, while custom software is tailored to satisfy specific user requirements for a particular organization. Custom software is not always developed from scratch (it can reuse components), and packaged software is not always cheaper—total costs depend on licensing, customization, and fit. Vertical-market packaged software is also widely available.","topic":"t-intro"},{"id":"A26-1-7","src":"A26","wk":1,"n":7,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following is not considered to be Embedded Software?","opts":["TV Remote Software","Nuclear Plant Control Software","Patient Management Software for a hospital","Robot Control Software","Automobile Control Software"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Patient Management Software for a hospital runs on general-purpose computers or servers and is used for administrative and clinical data management, making it an information system rather than embedded software. In contrast, TV remote software, nuclear plant control, robot control, and automobile control software all run on specialized hardware (microcontrollers or processors) within larger devices to perform specific control functions, which is the defining characteristic of embedded software.","topic":"t-intro"},{"id":"A26-1-8","src":"A26","wk":1,"n":8,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements regarding project management activities are correct?","opts":["Planning is performed during execution","Feasibility analysis generally precedes project planning","Project execution involves carrying out planned activities","Feasibility study and project execution are identical activities","A project manager may be involved in all three phases"],"ans":[1,2,4],"exp":"Feasibility analysis is conducted before detailed project planning to assess viability, and project execution is indeed the phase where planned activities are carried out. A project manager is typically involved across feasibility, planning, and execution phases. Planning is primarily done before execution (though some iterative planning may occur), and feasibility study and execution are completely distinct activities.","topic":"t-pm"},{"id":"A26-1-9","src":"A26","wk":1,"n":9,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following activities are typically associated with project planning?","opts":["Formally accepting project deliverables","Developing schedules for project activities","Conducting lessons-learned reviews","Allocating project resources","Releasing project resources after completion"],"ans":[1,3],"exp":"Developing schedules for project activities and allocating project resources are core project planning activities. Formally accepting deliverables, conducting lessons-learned reviews, and releasing resources all occur during project closing or execution phases, not during planning.","topic":"t-pm"},{"id":"A26-1-10","src":"A26","wk":1,"n":10,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following are benefits of defining milestones in a software project?","opts":["Automatic generation of source code","Elimination of the need for project planning","Effective addressing of manpower turnover","Improved progress tracking","Better visibility into project status"],"ans":[3,4],"exp":"Defining milestones improves progress tracking by providing clear checkpoints to measure achievement against the schedule, and it gives better visibility into project status for stakeholders. Milestones do not generate code, eliminate the need for planning, or directly address manpower turnover—they are management tools for monitoring and communication.","topic":"t-pm"},{"id":"A26-2-1","src":"A26","wk":2,"n":1,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements regarding the Waterfall model are correct?","opts":["Integration activities are distributed evenly throughout development","Customer feedback is typically obtained through frequent prototypes","Requirement changes after project initiation can be difficult to accommodate","The model may create a false sense of project progress","Working software may not be available until late in the project"],"ans":[2,3,4],"exp":"In the Waterfall model, requirements are fixed early, making late changes difficult and costly to accommodate. Since working software is delivered only at the end, progress is often measured by documents and completed phases, which can create a false sense of progress, and stakeholders do not see executable software until late in the project. Integration also happens at the end (not evenly distributed), and prototypes are not a standard part of Waterfall.","topic":"t-waterfall"},{"id":"A26-2-2","src":"A26","wk":2,"n":2,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following pairs are correctly matched in the V-Model of software development?","opts":["Requirements Specification ↔ System Testing","High-Level Design ↔ Integration Testing","Detailed Design ↔ Unit Testing","Project Planning ↔ Unit Testing","Coding ↔ System Testing"],"ans":[0,1,2],"exp":"In the V-Model, each development phase on the left has a corresponding testing phase on the right: Requirements Specification maps to System Testing, High-Level Design maps to Integration Testing, and Detailed Design maps to Unit Testing. Project Planning has no direct testing counterpart, and Coding corresponds to Unit Testing—not System Testing.","topic":"t-waterfall"},{"id":"A26-2-3","src":"A26","wk":2,"n":3,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following are commonly cited reasons for using prototyping?","opts":["Guaranteed reduction in project duration","Requirements are only partially known","Improved communication between users and developers","Elimination of all testing activities","Improved user involvement"],"ans":[1,2,4],"exp":"Prototyping is commonly used when requirements are unclear or only partially known, as it helps clarify them through tangible feedback. It also improves communication between users and developers by giving users something concrete to interact with, and it actively involves users in the refinement process. However, prototyping does not guarantee shorter project duration (it can add time) and does not eliminate the need for testing.","topic":"t-proto"},{"id":"A26-2-4","src":"A26","wk":2,"n":4,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following are major objectives of the RAD model?","opts":["Reduce software development time","Reduce software development cost","Delay requirement changes until after testing","Accommodate change requests as early as possible","Eliminate the need for user involvement"],"ans":[0,1,3],"exp":"The Rapid Application Development (RAD) model aims to reduce development time and cost through iterative prototyping, reusable components, and accelerated cycles. It also emphasizes accommodating change requests as early as possible, keeping the system flexible to user feedback. RAD does not delay changes until after testing—it welcomes early changes—and it relies heavily on continuous user involvement, not eliminates it.","topic":"t-proto"},{"id":"A26-2-5","src":"A26","wk":2,"n":5,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements about refactoring in the agile model are correct?","opts":["Refactoring requires changing user-visible behavior","Refactoring improves internal code structure","Refactoring should preserve external behavior","Refactoring is intended to add major new functionality","Refactoring can improve maintainability"],"ans":[1,2,4],"exp":"Refactoring improves the internal structure of the code without altering its external behavior, which preserves functionality while making the system cleaner and easier to maintain. It is not meant to add new features or change what the user sees—those are separate development activities.","topic":"t-agile"},{"id":"A26-2-6","src":"A26","wk":2,"n":6,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following are important practices in the XP model of software development?","opts":["Frequent integration instead of end-of-project integration","Ongoing refactoring and design improvement","Strict requirement freeze before development starts","Greater emphasis on feedback and adaptation","Testing only after implementation is complete"],"ans":[0,1,3],"exp":"Extreme Programming (XP) emphasizes frequent integration (often multiple times a day), continuous refactoring and design improvement to keep code clean, and strong feedback and adaptation through practices like test-driven development and customer involvement. XP does not freeze requirements early—it welcomes changing requirements—and it promotes testing before or alongside implementation, not only after.","topic":"t-agile"},{"id":"A26-2-7","src":"A26","wk":2,"n":7,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements are FALSE regarding Scrum Sprints?","opts":["A Sprint is a time-boxed development cycle","Software increments can be designed, coded, and tested within a Sprint","Sprint duration is exactly one month","Changes are normally entertained throughout the Sprint without restriction","Scrum projects progress through a series of Sprints"],"ans":[2,3],"exp":"Sprint duration is not fixed at exactly one month—it is typically 1 to 4 weeks, and the team chooses the length. Also, once a Sprint begins, the Sprint Backlog is fixed, and changes are not entertained without restriction; any change requires canceling the Sprint or deferring it to the next Sprint. The other statements (a, b, and e) are true regarding Scrum Sprints. <br>Lecture slides: sprint 'target duration is one month' (not exactly) and 'no changes entertained during a sprint'.","topic":"t-agile"},{"id":"A26-2-8","src":"A26","wk":2,"n":8,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements regarding the Product Backlog are correct?","opts":["It contains only completed tasks","It contains a list of desired work for the project","It may contain both story-based and task-based work items","It is prioritized by the Product Owner","It cannot be modified once the project starts"],"ans":[1,2,3],"exp":"The Product Backlog contains a list of all desired work for the project, including user stories, technical tasks, and bug fixes, and it is continuously prioritized by the Product Owner to maximize value. It is not limited to completed tasks—those are removed or marked done—and it is dynamic, meaning it can be refined, reprioritized, or updated throughout the project, not frozen at the start.","topic":"t-agile"},{"id":"A26-2-9","src":"A26","wk":2,"n":9,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following are typically represented in a Sprint Burn Down Chart?","opts":["Customer satisfaction ratings","Remaining effort or hours","Progress across Sprint days","Number of employees in the organization","Work remaining until Sprint completion"],"ans":[1,2,4],"exp":"A Sprint Burndown Chart tracks the remaining effort (e.g., hours or story points) across the days of the Sprint, showing how much work is left until completion. It does not display customer satisfaction, organizational headcount, or any metric outside the Sprint's work progress.","topic":"t-agile"},{"id":"A26-2-10","src":"A26","wk":2,"n":10,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements about Product Backlog prioritization are correct?","opts":["Backlog items are typically ordered according to priority","The Product Owner is responsible for backlog prioritization","Prioritization helps determine what work should be addressed first","All backlog items must have equal priority","Priorities cannot change during a project"],"ans":[0,1,2],"exp":"The Product Backlog is an ordered list where items are ranked by priority, and the Product Owner is solely responsible for this prioritization to ensure the team works on the most valuable items first. Prioritization directly guides the sequence of development, but items do not have equal priority—they vary in importance—and priorities can and do change frequently based on feedback, market conditions, or business needs.","topic":"t-agile"},{"id":"A26-3-1","src":"A26","wk":3,"n":1,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the followings is an example of development cost?","opts":["Salary to staffs","Hardware cost","Recruitment cost","Staff training cost","Operational cost"],"ans":[0],"exp":"Salaries of developers, testers, and other technical staff directly involved in building the product are considered direct development costs. Hardware used for development can sometimes be included, but it is often classified as infrastructure; recruitment and training are overheads, and operational costs occur after deployment.","topic":"t-bizcase"},{"id":"A26-3-2","src":"A26","wk":3,"n":2,"type":"msq","text":"Which element of Project Portfolio Management (PPM) is responsible for determining the scope of the portfolio, such as whether it should include all organizational projects or only projects from a specific domain (e.g., Healthcare Projects)?","opts":["portfolio definition","portfolio management","portfolio optimization","portfolio deletion","portfolio creation"],"ans":[0],"exp":"Portfolio definition is the PPM element responsible for establishing the portfolio's purpose, boundaries, and selection criteria—including deciding which types of projects (e.g., all organizational projects or only healthcare projects) are included. Portfolio management, optimization, deletion, and creation are related but focus on ongoing governance, value maximization, removal, or establishment, rather than defining scope.","topic":"t-bizcase"},{"id":"A26-3-3","src":"A26","wk":3,"n":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is generally classified as an indirect cost in a software project?","opts":["Insurance","Development cost","setup cost","operational cost","Hardware procurement cost"],"ans":[0],"exp":"Insurance is generally classified as an indirect cost (overhead) because it supports overall business operations and cannot be traced directly to a specific project. Development costs, setup costs, operational costs, and hardware procurement are typically treated as direct costs tied to specific project activities.","topic":"t-bizcase"},{"id":"A26-3-4","src":"A26","wk":3,"n":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the followings is an example of a variable cost?","opts":["Cost of computer forms according to the amount of processing","Straight-line depreciation of hardware","exempt employee salaries","insurance","Annual software license fee paid at a fixed rate"],"ans":[0],"exp":"The cost of computer forms varies directly with the amount of processing activity—more processing means more forms consumed, making it a true variable cost. In contrast, straight-line depreciation, exempt salaries, insurance, and fixed-rate software license fees remain constant regardless of usage volume, so they are fixed costs.","topic":"t-bizcase"},{"id":"A26-3-5","src":"A26","wk":3,"n":5,"type":"mcq","text":"In break-even analysis, return period is the period when","opts":["the candidate system provides greater benefit than the old one","the candidate system provides less benefit than the old one","the candidate system provides equal benefit as the old one","the candidate system does not provide any benefit","the old system does not provide any benefit"],"ans":[0],"exp":"The return period is defined as the time when the candidate system provides greater benefit than the old one — that is, the point at which the investment has not just broken even, but actually started delivering net positive advantage.","topic":"t-cba"},{"id":"A26-3-6","src":"A26","wk":3,"n":6,"type":"mcq","text":"What will be the ROI for the following project details?<div class=\"tblwrap\"><table class=\"mini\"><tr><th>Year</th><th>Cash flow, project B (Rs.)</th></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>−1,000,000</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>200,000</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>200,000</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>200,000</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>200,000</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>300,000</td></tr></table></div>","opts":["2%","2.5%","5%","10%","10.5%","12%","12.5%"],"ans":[0],"exp":"Total net profit = Rs. 100,000 Project duration = 5 years Average annual net profit = 100,000 / 5 = Rs. 20,000 Average annual ROI = (20,000 / 1,000,000) × 100 = 2%","topic":"t-cba"},{"id":"A26-3-7","src":"A26","wk":3,"n":7,"type":"msq","text":"In which of the following risk evaluation methods, it is required to construct a project risk matrix?","opts":["Risk identification and ranking","Risk and net present value","Cost-Benefit Analysis","Risk profile analysis","Using decision trees"],"ans":[0],"exp":"A project risk matrix (probability-impact matrix) is constructed during risk identification and ranking to visually prioritize risks based on their likelihood and potential impact. This helps determine which risks need immediate attention. Other methods like NPV analysis, cost-benefit analysis, risk profile analysis, or decision trees do not require constructing a risk matrix as their primary tool.","topic":"t-riskeval"},{"id":"A26-3-8","src":"A26","wk":3,"n":8,"type":"msq","text":"In Cost-Benefit Analysis method for risk evaluation, it is required","opts":["to categorize the projects as high, medium or low risk projects using a scoring method and assign an appropriate risk premium for each category","to evaluate whether the risk is important and, decide on a suitable course of action","to construct risk profiles using sensitive analysis","to construct a project risk matrix","to consider each possible outcome and estimate the probability of its occurring and the corresponding value of the outcome"],"ans":[4],"exp":"In Cost-Benefit Analysis (CBA) for risk evaluation, the standard approach is to consider each possible outcome, estimate its probability of occurrence, and assign a corresponding monetary value to it. This allows calculation of Expected Monetary Value (EMV), which adjusts the benefits and costs for risk. Constructing risk profiles, risk matrices, or categorizing projects by risk premium are associated with other risk techniques, not the core of CBA.","topic":"t-riskeval"},{"id":"A26-3-9","src":"A26","wk":3,"n":9,"type":"msq","text":"Innovative partnerships contain the projects","opts":["that an organization undertakes within a particular planning cycle","implementing a single strategy","performing the activities of identifying common infrastructure and its implementation and maintenance","involved in developing new innovative products based on some research","based on collaboration by different organizations"],"ans":[4],"exp":"Innovative partnerships are defined by collaboration between different organizations—such as companies, research institutions, or universities—to jointly develop new products, share risks, and combine resources and expertise. While option (d) mentions developing innovative products, it lacks the essential element of cross-organizational collaboration, which is the defining feature of innovative partnerships.","topic":"t-programme"},{"id":"A26-3-10","src":"A26","wk":3,"n":10,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements is NOT generally true about a programme manager managing multiple concurrent projects?","opts":["They are responsible for coordinating several projects at the same time","They typically maintain impersonal relationships with skilled resources","They seek to minimize the overall demand for resources across projects","They often view projects as having distinct objectives and characteristics","They strive to optimize the utilization of resources across concurrent projects"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Programme managers managing multiple concurrent projects generally build strong, collaborative, and personal relationships with skilled resources to secure their commitment, motivation, and availability—not impersonal ones. The other statements (a, c, d, and e) are generally true: they coordinate multiple projects, seek to minimize resource demand, view projects as distinct, and optimize resource utilization across the portfolio. Thus, (b) is the statement that is NOT generally true. <br><b>Watch out:</b> in the textbook table (Hughes, Cotterell & Mall), the programme manager has <i>personal</i> relationships with skilled resources, <i>optimises</i> resource use and sees projects as <i>similar</i>; 'minimise demand' and 'projects as unique' are project-manager traits. The official key marks only (b); if the exam asks this as an MSQ, (c) and (d) are also project-manager traits.","topic":"t-programme"},{"id":"A26-4-1","src":"A26","wk":4,"n":1,"type":"mcq","text":"How many hours is a person month?","opts":["122","132","142","152","162","172","720"],"ans":[3],"exp":"A person-month is a unit of effort representing the work done by one person in one month. COCOMO II and many project management standards assume 152 hours per person-month (based on 19 working days × 8 hours/day).","topic":"t-estbasics"},{"id":"A26-4-2","src":"A26","wk":4,"n":2,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following software effort estimation methods is most suitable when an existing software system is being modified or enhanced?","opts":["Parametric Models","Expert Judgment Technique","Analogy-Based Method","Price-to-Win Method","Similarity-Based Method"],"ans":[2],"exp":"When modifying or enhancing an existing system, the most suitable estimation method is one that compares the proposed changes to similar modifications made previously in the same system or in comparable projects. The Analogy-Based Method does exactly that — it uses historical data from past, similar work to estimate effort for the new changes.","topic":"t-esttech"},{"id":"A26-4-3","src":"A26","wk":4,"n":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is a key advantage of the Delphi estimation technique compared to estimation by a single expert?","opts":["It is largely free from the bias of any individual expert","It always produces a quantitative estimate","It explicitly accounts for project-specific peculiarities","It considers that different system components may have different complexities","It requires significantly less time and effort than expert estimation"],"ans":[0],"exp":"The Delphi estimation technique (including Wideband Delphi) involves multiple experts providing anonymous estimates in iterative rounds, with feedback and revision between rounds. Its key advantage over a single expert is that it reduces individual bias, groupthink, and the undue influence of dominant personalities, leading to a more balanced and reliable estimate.","topic":"t-esttech"},{"id":"A26-4-4","src":"A26","wk":4,"n":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the followings is NOT a limitation of algorithmic estimation models?","opts":["They lack effective mechanisms to account for exceptional situations, such as outstanding teamwork or an unusually good match between skills and tasks","They can be calibrated using data from past projects to improve estimation accuracy","Inaccurate size estimates or cost-driver ratings can lead to unreliable effort estimates","Some important factors, such as experience and team cohesion, are difficult to quantify accurately","They may not adequately capture qualitative aspects of the development environment"],"ans":[1],"exp":"It is actually an advantage or feature of algorithmic models. Calibration improves accuracy, which is a strength, not a drawback.","topic":"t-esttech"},{"id":"A26-4-5","src":"A26","wk":4,"n":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Consider a transaction project of 90,000 lines of code. Productivity is about 500 SLOC/person- month. What will be the effort required to develop the project?","opts":["60 person-month","90 person-month","120 person-month","180 person-month","240 person-month","300 person-month","500 person-month"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Effort = size / productivity = 90,000 SLOC ÷ 500 SLOC per person-month = 180 person-months.","topic":"t-esttech"},{"id":"A26-4-6","src":"A26","wk":4,"n":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the followings is NOT true regarding function points?","opts":["independent of the language, tools, or methodologies used for implementation","based on the user’s external view of the system","measure functionality that the user requests and receives","can be estimated early in analysis and design","computed from the code implementing the requirements"],"ans":[4],"exp":"Function points are a size metric based on the functional requirements of the system as seen from the user's perspective. They are intentionally designed to be independent of implementation details such as programming language, tools, or actual code.","topic":"t-fp"},{"id":"A26-4-7","src":"A26","wk":4,"n":7,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the followings is NOT a key component in function point analysis?","opts":["Number of Inputs","Number of Outputs","Number of Inquiries","Number of Interfaces","Number of Operations"],"ans":[4],"exp":"Number of Operations is not a standard component in function point analysis. \"Operations\" is more relevant to object-oriented metrics (e.g., number of methods) or UML, not to function points.","topic":"t-fp"},{"id":"A26-4-8","src":"A26","wk":4,"n":8,"type":"mcq","text":"In a software project, if the degree of influence (DI) for each of the 14 General System Characteristics is 5, then what will be value of VAF (Value Adjustment Factor), using Function Point analysis?","opts":["1.07","1.21","2.07","2.21","1.35","70"],"ans":[4],"exp":"VAF=0.65+70/100​=0.65+0.70=1.35 where, total degree influence, TDI=14×5=70","topic":"t-fp"},{"id":"A26-4-9","src":"A26","wk":4,"n":9,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the followings are limitations of function point analysis?","opts":["It does not consider algorithmic complexity of a function","It is hard to fudge","It is language independent","It is understandable by clients","It requires extensive training"],"ans":[0,4],"exp":"Function point analysis does not account for internal algorithmic complexity, making it less accurate for highly complex functions, and it demands significant training to apply consistently. In contrast, being language-independent, hard to manipulate, and understandable to clients are considered strengths, not limitations.","topic":"t-fp"},{"id":"A26-4-10","src":"A26","wk":4,"n":10,"type":"mcq","text":"Consider a project with the following functional units: 80 user inputs, 60 user outputs, 50 user enquiries, 10 user files, 7 external interfaces. What will be the unadjusted function points for the project?","opts":["769","869","969","1069","1169"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Using standard IFPUG average complexity weights: External Inputs (EI): 80 × 4 = 320 External Outputs (EO): 60 × 5 = 300 External Inquiries (EQ): 50 × 4 = 200 Internal Logical Files (ILF): 10 × 10 = 100 External Interface Files (EIF): 7 × 7 = 49 Total UFP = 320 + 300 + 200 + 100 + 49 = 969","topic":"t-fp"},{"id":"A26-5-1","src":"A26","wk":5,"n":1,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the followings are NOT examples of expert judgment-based technique?","opts":["Basic expert judgement","Weighted average estimating","Delphi cost estimation","COCOMO","Consensus estimating","Halstead’s software science"],"ans":[3,5],"exp":"Expert judgment techniques rely on human expertise, experience, and consensus—examples include basic expert judgement, weighted average estimating, Delphi, and consensus estimating. COCOMO is an algorithmic model based on mathematical formulas, and Halstead’s software science is an analytical metric based on operators and operands; neither uses expert judgment as their primary mechanism.","topic":"t-esttech"},{"id":"A26-5-2","src":"A26","wk":5,"n":2,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the followings are examples of organic project?","opts":["Accounting system","Compilers","Operating systems","Real-time systems","Student Information System","Linkers"],"ans":[0,4],"exp":"In the COCOMO model, organic projects are small, simple, and well-understood, with experienced teams and flexible requirements—typical examples include accounting systems and student information systems. Compilers and linkers are semi-detached, while operating systems and real-time systems are embedded due to their complexity and tight constraints.","topic":"t-cocomo"},{"id":"A26-5-3","src":"A26","wk":5,"n":3,"type":"msq","text":"In intermediate COCOMO, if there are stringent reliability requirements on the product, then the initial cost and effort estimates obtained by the basic COCOMO, are","opts":["scaled downwards","scaled upwards","remained unchanged","potentially decreased","halved"],"ans":[1],"exp":"In intermediate COCOMO, the cost driver RELY (required software reliability) has a value greater than 1.0 for stringent reliability requirements (e.g., High = 1.15, Very High = 1.30). This multiplies the basic COCOMO estimate by an Effort Adjustment Factor (EAF) &gt; 1.0, thus scaling the effort and cost upwards—not downward, unchanged, decreased, or halved.","topic":"t-cocomo"},{"id":"A26-5-4","src":"A26","wk":5,"n":4,"type":"msq","text":"A distributed decision support System (DSS) for an organization having offices at several places across the country has the following modules: a database module, a graphical user interface (GUI) module and a communication module. Which of the following COCOMO models will be best suitable for estimating the development cost?","opts":["Basic COCOMO","Intermediate COCOMO","COCOMO 81","Detailed (Complete) COCOMO","All of these"],"ans":[3],"exp":"A distributed DSS with database, GUI, and communication modules involves different types of components, each with its own complexity, cost drivers, and development environment. The Detailed (Complete) COCOMO model is best suited because it allows estimating effort module by module (with phase-level and module-level cost drivers) and then summing them, providing greater accuracy than Basic or Intermediate COCOMO, which apply uniform cost drivers to the entire project.","topic":"t-cocomo"},{"id":"A26-5-5","src":"A26","wk":5,"n":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following COCOMO II sub-models would be used when requirements are available but design has not yet started?","opts":["Application composition","Early design","Reuse","Post-architecture","Application configuration"],"ans":[1],"exp":"The Early design sub-model of COCOMO II is specifically used when requirements are known but the detailed software design and architecture have not yet been completed. It uses fewer cost drivers (7 instead of 17) and provides a coarse estimate based on function points or KSLOC. Application composition is for very early prototyping, reuse is for estimating effort to integrate reused components, and post-architecture applies once the architecture is fully defined.","topic":"t-cocomo2"},{"id":"A26-5-6","src":"A26","wk":5,"n":6,"type":"msq","text":"Post-architecture model of COCOMO II is primarily used for","opts":["systems developed using dynamic languages, DB programming etc","initial effort estimation based on system requirements and design options","computing the effort to integrate the reusable components or automatically generated code","estimating the development effort based on system design specification","Evaluating the impact of project cost drivers and scale factors on the final effort estimate"],"ans":[3,4],"exp":"The Post-Architecture model of COCOMO II is primarily used for estimating development effort based on the system design specification (d), as it leverages detailed size inputs and 17 cost drivers once the architecture is stable. Additionally, it serves to evaluate the impact of project cost drivers and scale factors (e) by allowing managers to adjust parameters like team capability or reliability requirements and observe their effect on the final effort estimate, making both options correct and complementary uses of the model.","topic":"t-cocomo2"},{"id":"A26-5-7","src":"A26","wk":5,"n":7,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following estimation techniques are based on making an educated guess of the project parameters?","opts":["Expert judgement","Basic COCOMO","Halstead’s software science","Delphi cost estimation","Intermediate COCOMO","Complete COCOMO"],"ans":[0,3],"exp":"Expert judgement and Delphi cost estimation rely on human expertise, experience, and consensus to make informed \"educated guesses\" about project parameters. In contrast, Basic COCOMO, Intermediate COCOMO, Complete COCOMO, and Halstead's software science are algorithmic or analytical models that use fixed mathematical formulas or metrics, not subjective human estimation. .","topic":"t-halstead"},{"id":"A26-5-8","src":"A26","wk":5,"n":8,"type":"msq","text":"In Halstead’s software science,","opts":["the programming effort varies inversely with the volume","the programming effort varies linearly with the volume","the programming effort varies as the square of the volume","the programming effort varies as the cube of the volume","the programming effort does not vary with the volume"],"ans":[2],"exp":"In Halstead's software science E = V/L and L = V*/V, so E = V²/V*: programming effort varies as the square of the volume V (V measures the information content from operators and operands).","topic":"t-halstead"},{"id":"A26-5-9","src":"A26","wk":5,"n":9,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is NOT a reason for late project becoming later due to addition of manpower?","opts":["Increased communication among team members","Learning by the added members","Increased project scope","Limited scope for parallelizing various project activities","Additional effort required from experienced team members to train and mentor new members"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Brooks' Law states that adding manpower to a late project makes it later due to factors like increased communication overhead, learning curves for new members, limited parallelizability of tasks, and training efforts required from experienced staff. Increased project scope, however, is not a consequence of adding manpower—it is a separate decision (scope creep) and not a reason why additional people delay the project.","topic":"t-staffing"},{"id":"A26-5-10","src":"A26","wk":5,"n":10,"type":"mcq","text":"Suppose the development time of a small software product has been estimated to be one and half year. Then, in order to develop the product in six months, what will be total increase in development effort? (Use Jensen's model).","opts":["3 times","6 times","9 times","12 times","18 times","27 times","81 times"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Jensen's model (a softened Putnam model) gives effort ∝ 1/td². Compressing 18 months to 6 months is a ratio of 3, so effort grows 3² = 9 times. (Putnam's model would give 3⁴ = 81 times.)","topic":"t-staffing"},{"id":"A26-6-1","src":"A26","wk":6,"n":1,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements regarding a Work Breakdown Structure (WBS) are correct?","opts":["A WBS represents a hierarchical decomposition of project work","A WBS decomposes tasks into smaller subtasks","A WBS shows the duration of each activity","A WBS helps organize project scope into manageable components","A WBS explicitly shows task dependencies"],"ans":[0,1,3],"exp":"A WBS is a hierarchical decomposition of the total project work into smaller, manageable components (work packages), which helps organize and define the project scope. It decomposes tasks into subtasks but does not show activity durations (that belongs to schedules like Gantt charts) or task dependencies (shown in network diagrams).","topic":"t-wbs"},{"id":"A26-6-2","src":"A26","wk":6,"n":2,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements regarding an Activity Network are correct?","opts":["It cannot represent concurrent activities","An Activity Network depicts the flow of project tasks","It is primarily used to define software requirements","It can show task dependencies","It can represent task sequence relationships"],"ans":[1,3,4],"exp":"An Activity Network (e.g., PERT or CPM) depicts the logical flow and sequence of project tasks, clearly showing task dependencies and relationships (e.g., finish-to-start). It can also represent concurrent activities using parallel paths, so option (a) is false. It is used for project scheduling, not for defining software requirements, making option (c) incorrect.","topic":"t-wbs"},{"id":"A26-6-3","src":"A26","wk":6,"n":3,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following pieces of information is typically represented in a WBS?","opts":["Major project deliverables","Critical path information","Subtasks associated with project activities","Activity durations","Hierarchical relationships among work items"],"ans":[0,2,4],"exp":"A WBS typically represents major project deliverables, subtasks (work packages) associated with project activities, and hierarchical relationships among work items. It does not include critical path information (shown in network diagrams) or activity durations (shown in schedules like Gantt charts).","topic":"t-wbs"},{"id":"A26-6-4","src":"A26","wk":6,"n":4,"type":"msq","text":"Which statements about the critical path are true?","opts":["It always includes the maximum number of activities","It is a path from project start to finish","It determines the minimum project duration","It always contains all project tasks","Delays on critical activities may delay the project"],"ans":[1,2,4],"exp":"The critical path is the longest sequence of dependent activities from project start to finish, and it determines the minimum project duration. Delaying any critical activity will delay the project unless corrective action is taken. It does not necessarily contain the maximum number of activities (duration matters more), and it never includes all project tasks—only those with zero slack.","topic":"t-cpm"},{"id":"A26-6-5","src":"A26","wk":6,"n":5,"type":"msq","text":"What can be the effect of the delay of an activity in the critical path?","opts":["The project completion date may be delayed","The critical path may change","The project duration is guaranteed to remain unchanged","The delay is always absorbed by slack time","Corrective actions may be required"],"ans":[0,1,4],"exp":"Delaying a critical-path activity directly threatens the project completion date (a), may cause the critical path to shift to another sequence of activities (b), and typically forces the project manager to take corrective actions such as crashing or fast-tracking to recover (e). Critical activities have zero slack, so the delay is never absorbed by slack time (d), and the project duration is not guaranteed to remain unchanged (c).","topic":"t-cpm"},{"id":"A26-6-6","src":"A26","wk":6,"n":6,"type":"msq","text":"Which activities have zero float (slack) in the network in the PERT chart shown?","opts":["A","B","E (Recruit staff)","F (Draft office procedures)","G (Install and test)"],"ans":[2,3],"exp":"E (Recruit staff) ES=0, DUR=10, EF=10 LS=0, SL=0, LF=10 F (Draft office procedures) ES=10, DUR=3, EF=13 LS=10, SL=0, LF=13","topic":"t-cpm","fig":"brigette"},{"id":"A26-6-7","src":"A26","wk":6,"n":7,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following activities are on the critical path in the PERT chart diagram?","opts":["A","E (Recruit staff)","F (Draft office procedures)","C","G"],"ans":[1,2],"exp":"The critical path is the longest path from start to finish with zero slack: E (Recruit staff) → F (Draft office procedures). No other activities are on the critical path because they have positive slack and are not required to be completed on the critical timeline. .","topic":"t-cpm","fig":"brigette"},{"id":"A26-6-8","src":"A26","wk":6,"n":8,"type":"msq","text":"Suppose activity D is delayed by 2 weeks while all other activities remain unchanged in the PERT chart diagram. Which statements are true?","opts":["Activity G must start at week 11","D will finish at week 10","The project completion time remains 13 weeks","D will still not be on the critical path","The float of D becomes 1 week"],"ans":[1,2,3],"exp":"D will finish at 10 (8+2) where the completion will remain 13 as both EF =LF=13, and D will still not be on the critical path.","topic":"t-cpm","fig":"brigette"},{"id":"A26-6-9","src":"A26","wk":6,"n":9,"type":"msq","text":"Which statements about CPM/PERT are correct?","opts":["Activities with larger durations always have larger float","Forward pass computes earliest times","All activities can have non-zero float","Critical-path activities have positive float","Backward pass computes latest times","Float = LS − ES"],"ans":[1,4,5],"exp":"In CPM/PERT, the forward pass calculates earliest start and finish times (b), while the backward pass calculates latest start and finish times (e). Float (slack) is correctly computed as LS − ES (or LF − EF) (f). Larger duration does not guarantee larger float (a), critical activities have zero float (d), and not all activities can have non-zero float—critical ones have zero (c).","topic":"t-cpm"},{"id":"A26-6-10","src":"A26","wk":6,"n":10,"type":"msq","text":"Given an activity with Earliest Start (ES) = 5 days, Latest Finish (LF) = 30 days, and Duration = 10 days, which of the following are true?","opts":["Earliest Finish (EF) = 15 days","Float = 20 days","Latest Start (LS) = 20 days","Float = 15 days","Latest Start (LS) = 25 day"],"ans":[0,2,3],"exp":"EF = ES + Duration = 5 + 10 = 15 days → (a) is true. LS = LF − Duration = 30 − 10 = 20 days → (c) is true, (e) is false. Float = LS − ES = 20 − 5 = 15 days → (d) is true, (b) is false. Thus, the correct statements are a, c, and d.","topic":"t-cpm"},{"id":"A26-7-1","src":"A26","wk":7,"n":1,"type":"msq","text":"If the optimistic estimate is 4 days, the most likely estimate is 10 days, and the pessimistic estimate is 16 days, which of the following statements are correct?","opts":["Expected time is 10 days","Variance is 4","Standard deviation is 2","Expected time is 12 days","Variance is 2"],"ans":[0,1,2],"exp":"Expected time (TE) = (O + 4M + P) / 6 = (4 + 4×10 + 16) / 6 = 60/6 = 10 days → (a) is true, (d) is false. Variance (V) = ((P − O) / 6)² = ((16 − 4) / 6)² = (12/6)² = 2² = 4 → (b) is true, (e) is false. Standard deviation (SD) = √V = √4 = 2 → (c) is true.","topic":"t-pert"},{"id":"A26-7-2","src":"A26","wk":7,"n":2,"type":"msq","text":"A project manager is asked to deliver a project earlier than planned. Which actions are consistent with project crashing?","opts":["Increase the duration of critical activities","Add more skilled personnel to critical tasks","Use faster equipment or tools for critical activities","Reduce the scope of the project","Assign overtime to critical-path activities"],"ans":[1,2,4],"exp":"Project crashing is a schedule compression technique that aims to shorten the project duration without changing scope by adding resources or increasing efficiency, typically on critical-path activities. Adding skilled personnel (b), using faster tools/equipment (c), and assigning overtime (e) are classic crashing actions. Increasing activity duration (a) would lengthen the project, and reducing scope (d) is not part of standard crashing—it is scope reduction, not crashing.","topic":"t-pert"},{"id":"A26-7-3","src":"A26","wk":7,"n":3,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following conclusions are consistent with the Hawthorne Effect?","opts":["Human factors play a role in workplace productivity","Worker productivity is independent of observation","Employee performance can be influenced by perceived interest from management","Lighting conditions are the sole determinant of productivity","Being part of a study may affect worker behavior"],"ans":[0,2,4],"exp":"The Hawthorne Effect shows that workers' productivity is influenced by being observed and by perceived management interest, not just by physical conditions like lighting. This confirms that human factors (a), management attention (c), and awareness of being studied (e) all affect behavior. Observation does affect productivity (b is false), and lighting is not the sole determinant (d is false).","topic":"t-people"},{"id":"A26-7-4","src":"A26","wk":7,"n":4,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements about a functional organization are true?","opts":["Functional organizations eliminate the need for management layers","Functional groups often follow a pyramidal structure","Functional organizations discourage technical specialization","Functional organizations are commonly found in military, police, and hospitals","Individuals are grouped according to specialized functions"],"ans":[1,3,4],"exp":"Functional organizations group individuals by specialized functions (e.g., engineering, marketing) and typically follow a pyramidal hierarchy with multiple management layers. They are common in military, police, and hospitals. They do not eliminate management layers (a), nor do they discourage technical specialization—they actually encourage it (c).","topic":"t-org"},{"id":"A26-7-5","src":"A26","wk":7,"n":5,"type":"msq","text":"According to Belbin, which of the following statements are correct?","opts":["An eligible candidate has the required qualifications","A suitable candidate is capable of performing the job effectively","Eligibility and suitability always imply the same thing","A person may be eligible but not suitable","A suitable candidate can never lack formal qualifications"],"ans":[0,1,3],"exp":"Belbin distinguishes eligibility (formal qualifications, experience) from suitability (behavioral attributes, team fit, and capability to perform effectively). A candidate can be eligible but not suitable (e.g., highly qualified but poor collaborator), and eligibility and suitability are not the same. Suitability does not require formal qualifications, so option (e) is false. Thus, a, b, and d are correct.","topic":"t-people"},{"id":"A26-7-6","src":"A26","wk":7,"n":6,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements are consistent with Maslow's theory of motivation?","opts":["Food and shelter are examples of higher-level needs","Human needs can be arranged in a hierarchy","Higher-level needs emerge as lower-level needs are fulfilled","All individuals are motivated by exactly the same needs","Self-actualization is at the highest level of the hierarchy"],"ans":[1,2,4],"exp":"Maslow's theory proposes a hierarchy of human needs, where lower-level needs (physiological, safety) must be substantially satisfied before higher-level needs (esteem, self- actualization) emerge as motivators. Food and shelter are lower-level, not higher-level (a), and individuals differ in which needs they are pursuing (d). Self-actualization is indeed the highest level (e). Thus, b, c, and e are correct.","topic":"t-people"},{"id":"A26-7-7","src":"A26","wk":7,"n":7,"type":"msq","text":"Which statements correctly distinguish organization structure from team structure?","opts":["Organization structure focuses on teams within the organization","Team structure focuses on individuals within a team","Team structure determines how organizations are divided into departments","Organization structure and team structure operate at different levels","Team structure is unrelated to the arrangement of individuals"],"ans":[0,1,3],"exp":"Organization structure operates at the macro level, defining how the entire enterprise is divided into departments, reporting lines, and teams—making option (a) correct. Team structure works at the micro level, focusing on the roles and relationships of individuals within a specific team (b), and the two structures function at fundamentally different levels of analysis (d). <br><i>The official solution itself flagged option (a) as doubtful.</i>","topic":"t-org"},{"id":"A26-7-8","src":"A26","wk":7,"n":8,"type":"msq","text":"According to Herzberg, motivators are factors that:","opts":["Make the job feel worthwhile","Create a sense of achievement","Eliminate all dissatisfaction","Increase job satisfaction","Include working conditions"],"ans":[0,1,3],"exp":"According to Herzberg's Two-Factor Theory, motivators (intrinsic factors like achievement, recognition, and meaningful work) make the job feel worthwhile (a), create a sense of achievement (b), and directly increase job satisfaction (d). They do not eliminate dissatisfaction— that is the role of hygiene factors (c), and working conditions are hygiene factors, not motivators (e).","topic":"t-people"},{"id":"A26-7-9","src":"A26","wk":7,"n":9,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements are consistent with the Oldham–Hackman model?","opts":["A job requiring multiple skills may be perceived as more meaningful","Seeing a whole piece of work contributes to task identity","Understanding the importance of one's work relates to task significance","Motivation depends solely on financial rewards","Feedback can enhance job satisfaction"],"ans":[0,1,4],"exp":"In the Oldham–Hackman model, skill variety (a) makes work feel more meaningful by engaging multiple talents, task identity (b) enhances meaning by allowing workers to see a complete, identifiable piece of work, and feedback (e) directly improves job satisfaction by providing clear information about performance. These are three of the five core job characteristics that foster intrinsic motivation, while the model does not rely on financial rewards alone, and task significance is specifically about the job's impact on others—not merely understanding importance. <br><i>The official solution flagged option (c) as doubtful: task significance is about the impact of the job on other people, not just 'understanding importance'.</i>","topic":"t-people"},{"id":"A26-7-10","src":"A26","wk":7,"n":10,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following are ways in which good project management can reduce stress?","opts":["Providing realistic effort estimates","Reducing unexpected crises through better control","Increasing uncertainty about responsibilities","Reducing role ambiguity","Encouraging conflicting responsibilities"],"ans":[0,1,3],"exp":"Good project management reduces stress by setting realistic effort estimates (a), minimizing unexpected crises through proactive control (b), and clarifying roles to reduce ambiguity (d). In contrast, increasing uncertainty about responsibilities (c) and encouraging conflicting responsibilities (e) would heighten stress, not reduce it.","topic":"t-people"},{"id":"A26-8-1","src":"A26","wk":8,"n":1,"type":"mcq","text":"In the context of resource allocation, which one of the followings is true regarding resource schedule?","opts":["It indicates the planned start and completion dates for each activity","It indicates the actual start and completion dates for each activity","It shows the dates on which each resource will be required and level of that requirement","It shows the planned cumulative expenditure incurred by the use of resources over time","It shows the actual expenditure incurred by the use of resources over time","It usually shows the weekly or monthly costs over the life of the project"],"ans":[2],"exp":"A resource schedule specifies when each resource (e.g., personnel, equipment, materials) is needed and at what quantity or intensity over the project timeline. It focuses on resource availability and usage, not activity start/end dates (a), actual dates (b), cumulative expenditure (d), actual costs (e), or periodic cost breakdowns (f)—those belong to activity schedules, cost baselines, or financial reports.","topic":"t-resource"},{"id":"A26-8-2","src":"A26","wk":8,"n":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the followings indicates the planned start and completion dates for each activity?","opts":["Activity schedule","Resource schedule","Cost schedule","Time schedule","Program schedule"],"ans":[0],"exp":"An activity schedule (often represented as a Gantt chart or project calendar) shows the planned start and completion dates for each individual activity in the project. A resource schedule shows resource requirements over time, a cost schedule tracks costs, and program schedule refers to a broader set of related projects—none of which focus specifically on activity start/end dates. Thus, (a) is the correct answer.","topic":"t-resource"},{"id":"A26-8-3","src":"A26","wk":8,"n":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is not TRUE about resource histograms?","opts":["A resource histogram is a representation of the distribution of the resources required over the duration of the project","The ideal resource histogram should be smooth with an initial build-up and a staged run-down","Based on the resource histogram, some activities may be delayed to reduce the maximum demand of a resource","A resource histogram is used to estimate activity durations","The initial activity network is refined based on the resource histogram"],"ans":[3],"exp":"A resource histogram displays the distribution of resource requirements over time, helps in resource leveling (smoothing demand), and can lead to refining the activity network. However, it is not used to estimate activity durations—durations are estimated first using effort, productivity, and dependencies. The histogram is derived from the schedule, not used to create it. Thus, (d) is the statement that is NOT true.","topic":"t-resource"},{"id":"A26-8-4","src":"A26","wk":8,"n":4,"type":"msq","text":"There is a need to prioritize the activities of a project, because","opts":["finding the best allocation of resources is time consuming and difficult","allocating a resource to one activity limits the flexibility for resource allocation and scheduling of other activities","resources can be allocated to competing activities in some rational order","the lower-priority activities can be made to fit around the more critical, already scheduled activities","activity prioritization eliminates the need for resource-constrained scheduling"],"ans":[0,1,2,3],"exp":"Prioritizing activities is necessary because resource allocation is complex and time-consuming (a), assigning a resource to one task reduces flexibility for others (b), it provides a rational order for competing activities (c), and lower-priority tasks can then be scheduled around critical ones (d). However, prioritization does not eliminate the need for resource-constrained scheduling—it only helps manage it; constraints still exist and must be addressed (e is false).","topic":"t-resource"},{"id":"A26-8-5","src":"A26","wk":8,"n":5,"type":"mcq","text":"The expenditures incurred in an organization, which cannot be directly related to individual projects or jobs, are known as","opts":["Staff cost","Overheads","Usage charges","Service charges","Materials cost"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Overheads are indirect costs that cannot be traced directly to a specific project or job—they support overall operations (e.g., rent, utilities, administrative salaries, insurance). Staff costs, usage charges, service charges, and materials costs are typically direct costs tied to specific activities or projects. Thus, (b) is correct.","topic":"t-costsched"},{"id":"A26-8-6","src":"A26","wk":8,"n":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the followings is the proper scheduling sequence?","opts":["activity plan, resource allocation, risk assessment, cost schedule","activity plan, risk assessment, cost schedule, resource allocation","activity plan, risk assessment, resource allocation, cost schedule","activity plan, cost schedule, risk assessment, resource allocation","activity plan, resource allocation, cost schedule, risk assessment"],"ans":[2],"exp":"The logical sequence begins with the activity plan (defining tasks, durations, and dependencies), followed by risk assessment to identify uncertainties that could affect scheduling or resources. Next, resource allocation assigns people and equipment to activities (often requiring adjustments), and finally, the cost schedule is developed based on the allocated resources and timelines. This order ensures that risks are considered before committing resources, and costs are calculated after resource needs are known.","topic":"t-costsched"},{"id":"A26-8-7","src":"A26","wk":8,"n":7,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the followings are examples of oral formal regular reporting method?","opts":["Weekly progress meetings","Monthly progress meetings","Through progress reports","Through exception reports","Social interaction"],"ans":[0,1],"exp":"Oral formal regular reporting involves spoken, structured, and scheduled communication—weekly and monthly progress meetings fit this category. Progress reports and exception reports are written, not oral, and social interaction is informal, not formal. Thus, only (a) and (b) are correct.","topic":"t-monitor"},{"id":"A26-8-8","src":"A26","wk":8,"n":8,"type":"msq","text":"Canteen discussion is an example of which category of reporting method?","opts":["Oral formal regular","Oral formal adhoc","Written formal regular","Written formal adhoc","Oral informal adhoc"],"ans":[4],"exp":"A canteen discussion is spoken (oral), lacks any formal structure or agenda (informal), and happens spontaneously without a fixed schedule (adhoc). Therefore, it falls under oral informal adhoc reporting. It is neither formal, written, nor regular, making all other options incorrect.","topic":"t-monitor"},{"id":"A26-8-9","src":"A26","wk":8,"n":9,"type":"mcq","text":"In Red/Amber/Green reporting, Green represents which one of the followings?","opts":["The tasks are ‘on target’","The tasks are ‘not on target but recoverable’","The tasks are ‘not on target and recoverable only with difficulty’","The tasks are ‘not on target and not recoverable’","The tasks have been completed but are awaiting formal approval"],"ans":[0],"exp":"In Red/Amber/Green (RAG) status reporting, Green indicates that tasks are progressing as planned and on target. Amber means \"not on target but recoverable,\" while Red means \"not on target and recoverable only with difficulty\" or \"not recoverable.\" Thus, (a) is the correct answer.","topic":"t-monitor"},{"id":"A26-8-10","src":"A26","wk":8,"n":10,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the followings is the proper sequence of activities in the review process?","opts":["Planning, Review preparation, Review meeting, Rework and follow up","Planning, Review meeting, Review preparation, Rework and follow up","Review preparation, Planning, Review meeting, Rework and follow up","Review preparation, Review meeting, Planning, Rework and follow up","Review meeting, Review preparation, Planning, Rework and follow up"],"ans":[0],"exp":"The standard review process follows a logical sequence: Planning (define objectives, select reviewers, schedule), Review preparation (participants examine the work product individually), Review meeting (team discusses findings and identifies defects), and finally Rework and follow up (author fixes issues and moderator verifies corrections). Any other order, such as holding the meeting before preparation or planning, is incorrect. Thus, (a) is the proper sequence.","topic":"t-review"},{"id":"A26-9-1","src":"A26","wk":9,"n":1,"type":"msq","text":"When is code review performed during software life cycle?","opts":["After unit testing","After coding and compiling","During integration testing","During system testing","During acceptance testing"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Code review is a static testing technique performed after coding and compiling but before dynamic testing (such as unit testing). It is done to find defects early by examining the source code without executing it. Performing it later—during or after integration, system, or acceptance testing—would be too late in the lifecycle. Thus, (b) is the correct answer.","topic":"t-codereview"},{"id":"A26-9-2","src":"A26","wk":9,"n":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Cleanroom technique does not use which one of the following activities?","opts":["Code walkthrough","Code inspection","Formal verification for bug removal","Testing of code by executing it","Statistical quality control"],"ans":[3],"exp":"The Cleanroom software engineering technique emphasizes defect prevention through formal verification (mathematical correctness), code walkthroughs and inspections (static reviews), and statistical quality control (usage-based reliability certification). It deliberately avoids execution- based testing during development—the team does not run the code to find bugs; instead, they rely on formal methods and statistical testing by a separate team for certification. Thus, (d) is the activity not used in Cleanroom.","topic":"t-codereview"},{"id":"A26-9-3","src":"A26","wk":9,"n":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is true of a code review meeting?","opts":["It seeks to identify errors, the person(s) responsible for the identified errors, and the solutions for the identified errors","It seeks to identify errors and the person(s) responsible for the identified errors, but does not seek solutions","It seeks to identify errors, but does not seek solutions or identify the person(s) responsible for the identified errors","It seeks to identify errors and their cause as well as outlines the solutions","It seeks to identify errors as well as recommends solutions for the same, but does not attribute blame for the error to anyone in particular"],"ans":[2],"exp":"A code review meeting focuses on finding defects in the work product, not on fixing them or assigning blame. The goal is to improve quality by identifying errors, while solutions are developed later during rework, and responsibility is not attributed to individuals—the review is about the product, not the producer. Thus, (c) is the correct statement.","topic":"t-codereview"},{"id":"A26-9-4","src":"A26","wk":9,"n":4,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following charts records and displays the way in which targets have changed throughout the duration of the project?","opts":["Gantt chart","Pie chart","Timeline chart","PERT chart","Flow chart"],"ans":[2],"exp":"A timeline chart records and displays how project targets, such as milestone dates or deadlines, have changed over the course of the project. It visually tracks the evolution of planned versus actual dates, making it useful for monitoring schedule slips. Gantt charts, PERT charts, pie charts, and flow charts do not serve this specific purpose. Thus, (c) is the correct answer according to your book's terminology.","topic":"t-visual"},{"id":"A26-9-5","src":"A26","wk":9,"n":5,"type":"mcq","text":"The total value credited to a project at any point is known as","opts":["the planned value","the earned value","the actual cost","the average cost","the optimal cost","total value"],"ans":[1],"exp":"In Earned Value Management (EVM), Earned Value (EV) is the value of work actually completed at a given point in time, representing the budgeted cost of that completed work. Planned Value (PV) is the value of work scheduled, Actual Cost (AC) is what has been spent, and the other options are not standard EVM terms. Thus, (b) is correct.","topic":"t-eva"},{"id":"A26-9-6","src":"A26","wk":9,"n":6,"type":"mcq","text":"As part of the implementation of an Employee Payroll Information System project, a number of employee data records have to be manually typed into a database. Which one of the following techniques will be most appropriate for assigning earned value to this project?","opts":["The 0/100 technique","The 50/50 technique","The 75/25 technique","The 25/75 technique","The 100/100 technique","The milestone technique","The percentage complete technique"],"ans":[6],"exp":"Manually typing a large number of employee records into a database is a repetitive, quantifiable task where progress can be measured incrementally (e.g., records completed / total records). The percentage complete technique assigns earned value proportionally as work progresses—if 40% of records are typed, 40% of the budgeted value is earned. Fixed- ratio techniques (0/100, 50/50, etc.) or milestone techniques are better suited for discrete, non-divisible deliverables, not for continuous, measurable tasks like data entry. Thus, (g) is the most appropriate choice.","topic":"t-eva"},{"id":"A26-9-7","src":"A26","wk":9,"n":7,"type":"msq","text":"Suppose the work with a PV of Rs. 55,000 should have been completed by now. Some of that work has not been done, so the EV is only Rs. 45,000. What is the value of Schedule Variance (SV)?","opts":["Rs. 10,000","- Rs. 10,000","Rs. 1,00,000","Rs. 5,000","- Rs. 5,000"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Schedule Variance (SV) is calculated as: SV=EV−PV Given: EV = Rs. 45,000 PV = Rs. 55,000 SV=45,000−55,000=−10,000","topic":"t-eva"},{"id":"A26-9-8","src":"A26","wk":9,"n":8,"type":"msq","text":"Suppose, the work with a PV of Rs. 50,000 should have been completed by now. In fact, some of that work has not been done, so that the EV is only Rs. 40,000. Also, it is given that Rs. 60,000 had actually been spent to get this EV. What is the value of Schedule Performance Index (SPI)?","opts":["0.67","0.75","0.80","1.25","1.20","0.83"],"ans":[2],"exp":"The Schedule Performance Index (SPI) is calculated as: SPI=EV/PV​ Given: EV = Rs. 40,000 PV = Rs. 50,000 SPI=40,000/50,000=0.80","topic":"t-eva"},{"id":"A26-9-9","src":"A26","wk":9,"n":9,"type":"msq","text":"A negative CV means that,","opts":["the project is over cost","the project is below cost","the project is as per the planned cost","The project is having moderate cost","no effect on cost"],"ans":[0],"exp":"In Earned Value Management (EVM), Cost Variance (CV) is calculated as CV = EV − AC. A negative CV means that the actual cost (AC) is higher than the earned value (EV), indicating that the project is over budget (over cost).","topic":"t-eva"},{"id":"A26-9-10","src":"A26","wk":9,"n":10,"type":"msq","text":"Assume that a medium-sized project is experiencing considerable schedule slippage. Which of the followings may not help to get the project back on schedule?","opts":["Shortening the critical path","Altering the activity precedence requirements","Speeding up non-critical path activities","Reducing scope of the project","Reducing the quality of the product"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Speeding up non-critical path activities does not help recover schedule slippage because these activities have slack/float and do not affect the project's overall completion date—only the critical path determines the project duration. Shortening the critical path (a), altering precedence (b), reducing scope (d), or even temporarily reducing quality (e) could potentially shorten the schedule, though reducing quality is unethical and not recommended. Thus, (c) is the action that may not help.","topic":"t-backontrack"},{"id":"A26-10-1","src":"A26","wk":10,"n":1,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following work products (such as code, design document, code, etc.) associated with the software continually change during the development as well as the maintenance phase?","opts":["SRS document","Data Flow Diagrams","UML Diagrams","Code","Test cases","All of these"],"ans":[5],"exp":"During both development and maintenance, all software work products—SRS documents, Data Flow Diagrams, UML diagrams, code, and test cases—are subject to continual change. Requirements evolve, designs are refined, code is updated for bug fixes or enhancements, and test cases are modified to reflect new functionality or changes. Thus, all of these work products change throughout the software life cycle, making (f) the correct answer.","topic":"t-scm"},{"id":"A26-10-2","src":"A26","wk":10,"n":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is an important purpose of software configuration management?","opts":["To support efficient customer relationship management such as providing technical help and accepting bug reports","To avoid maintaining multiple copies of the same project files","To Keep track of which team member would perform which task and by when","To manage the workflow across the team members","To carry out feasibility study of the software to be developed"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Software Configuration Management (SCM) is primarily concerned with controlling changes and maintaining integrity of software artifacts. A key purpose is to avoid duplication and confusion by centrally managing versions of project files, preventing multiple uncontrolled copies. Customer relationship management (a), task assignment (c), workflow management (d), and feasibility studies (e) are outside the scope of SCM. Thus, (b) is correct.","topic":"t-scm"},{"id":"A26-10-3","src":"A26","wk":10,"n":3,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the followings are usually NOT a work product category associated with a software development process?","opts":["Uncontrolled","Pre-controlled","Controlled","Post-controlled","Semi-controlled"],"ans":[3,4],"exp":"Standard SCM categories are Uncontrolled, Pre-controlled, and Controlled — representing the state of control over work products. Post-controlled is sometimes used informally but is not a core SCM category, and Semi-controlled is not a recognized term in formal SCM frameworks. Thus, both are usually NOT considered standard work product categories.","topic":"t-scm"},{"id":"A26-10-4","src":"A26","wk":10,"n":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following statements is NOT correct regarding SCCS tool?","opts":["It stands for Source Code Control System","It is a popular configuration management tool on UNIX systems","It can be used for controlling and managing different versions of text files","It can handle binary files","It provides an efficient way of storing versions that minimize the amount of occupied disk space"],"ans":[3],"exp":"SCCS (Source Code Control System) is a UNIX-based version control tool designed primarily for text files (like source code). It stores changes efficiently by saving deltas (differences) between versions, which minimizes disk space. However, it does not handle binary files well—binary diffs are not efficiently stored, and SCCS is not suited for binary data. Thus, (d) is the statement that is NOT correct.","topic":"t-scm"},{"id":"A26-10-5","src":"A26","wk":10,"n":5,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following project management tools do not support portfolio management?","opts":["Ganttproject","Microsoft Project","ProjectLibre Desktop","Primavera","None of these"],"ans":[0,2],"exp":"GanttProject and ProjectLibre Desktop are desktop scheduling tools focused on individual project planning and Gantt charts; they lack strategic portfolio management capabilities.","topic":"t-scm"},{"id":"A26-10-6","src":"A26","wk":10,"n":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A system / software which is bought ‘as is’, is known as","opts":["Bespoke system","Off-the-shelf package","Customized off-the-shelf (COTS) software","Open source software","Free software"],"ans":[1],"exp":"An off-the-shelf package is a software product purchased \"as is,\" with no modifications or only minimal configuration, ready for immediate use. A bespoke system is custom-built, customized COTS involves modifications, and open source/free software refer to licensing, not the \"as is\" purchase model. Thus, (b) is correct.","topic":"t-contract"},{"id":"A26-10-7","src":"A26","wk":10,"n":7,"type":"msq","text":"In fixed price per unit delivered contracts, how the price is calculated?","opts":["The price is fixed when the contract is signed","The customer is charged at a fixed rate per unit of effort","Function Point (FP) counting is used for calculating the price to be quoted","The customer is charged based on the expected time to complete the project and the volume of materials needed","The price varies dynamically"],"ans":[2],"exp":"In a fixed price per unit delivered contract, the total price is determined by multiplying the number of functional units (e.g., function points) by a fixed rate per unit. Function Point counting is commonly used to measure the size of the software to be delivered, which then forms the basis for quoting the price. The price is not fixed upfront (a), nor is it based on effort (b), time and materials (d), or dynamic variation (e). Thus, (c) is correct.","topic":"t-contract"},{"id":"A26-10-8","src":"A26","wk":10,"n":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following occurs in restricted tendering process?","opts":["Any supplier can bid in response to the invitation to tender","Bids are received only from those suppliers who have been specifically invited","Bids are received only from the local suppliers","Negotiation is made with only one supplier","Negotiation is made with a group of suppliers"],"ans":[1],"exp":"In restricted tendering (also called selective tendering), only a limited number of pre-qualified suppliers are invited to submit bids. This contrasts with open tendering (a), where any supplier can bid; local supplier restrictions (c) are not the basis; and negotiation with one supplier (d) or a group (e) describes single-source or negotiated tendering, not restricted tendering. Thus, (b) is correct.","topic":"t-contract"},{"id":"A26-10-9","src":"A26","wk":10,"n":9,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following are NOT services?","opts":["Equipment","Training","Documentation","Consumables","Transitional insurance arrangements","Providing telecommunication facilities","All of these"],"ans":[0,3],"exp":"Services are intangible activities done for the client (training, documentation, insurance arrangements, telecom facilities). Equipment and consumables are tangible goods, so (a) and (d) are NOT services. The official key text printed 'c. Consumables' but consumables is option (d); the solution itself says (a) and (d).","topic":"t-contract"},{"id":"A26-10-10","src":"A26","wk":10,"n":10,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following are important reasons for not properly closing the projects?","opts":["Lack of interest by the project team","Underestimation of how fast know-how can get lost","Emotional factors","Indecision regarding project closure","Availability of a well-defined project closure process"],"ans":[0,1,2,3],"exp":"Projects often fail to close properly due to team disinterest (a), underestimation of knowledge loss (b), emotional attachment (c), and indecision about when or how to close (d). In contrast, a well- defined project closure process (e) helps ensure proper closure—it is a solution, not a cause of improper closure. Thus, (a), (b), (c), and (d) are correct.","topic":"t-closeout"},{"id":"A26-11-1","src":"A26","wk":11,"n":1,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following are desirable characteristics of good software?","opts":["Huge size of code base","Correctness","Reliability","Usability","Hardware dependence"],"ans":[1,2,3],"exp":"Desirable characteristics of good software include correctness (performs functions accurately), reliability (operates without failure under specified conditions), and usability (easy to learn and use). A huge code base (a) indicates complexity and higher maintenance costs, and hardware dependence (e) reduces portability—both are undesirable.","topic":"t-quality"},{"id":"A26-11-2","src":"A26","wk":11,"n":2,"type":"msq","text":"Which statements regarding ISO 9001 are correct?","opts":["It mandates the use of a specific programming language","It guarantees defect-free products","It emphasizes management responsibility","It requires a quality system to be established","It includes process control requirements"],"ans":[2,3,4],"exp":"ISO 9001 is a quality management system standard that focuses on processes, not specific technical outcomes. It emphasizes management responsibility (c), requires a documented quality system (d), and includes process control requirements (e). It does not mandate programming languages (a) or guarantee defect-free products (b).","topic":"t-iso"},{"id":"A26-11-3","src":"A26","wk":11,"n":3,"type":"msq","text":"Which statements about ISO 9001 certification are correct?","opts":["Certification indicates that documented procedures are followed","Certification automatically improves software reliability","Certification alone does not guarantee product quality","A certified organization may still have an ineffective process","Certification removes the need for management oversight"],"ans":[0,2,3],"exp":"ISO 9001 certification confirms that an organization follows documented procedures (a), but it does not guarantee product quality (c) or reliability (b)—it only ensures processes are defined and followed, not that they are optimal. A certified organization can still have ineffective processes (d), and certification does not remove the need for management oversight (e)—in fact, it emphasizes management responsibility.","topic":"t-iso"},{"id":"A26-11-4","src":"A26","wk":11,"n":4,"type":"msq","text":"Which statements about the Initial (Level 1) maturity level are correct?","opts":["Processes are typically ad hoc and chaotic","Processes are quantitatively managed","Continuous process improvement is institutionalized","Success depends heavily on individual effort","It is the lowest maturity level"],"ans":[0,3,4],"exp":"At CMM Level 1 (Initial), processes are ad hoc, chaotic, and unpredictable (a), success depends heavily on individual effort rather than repeatable processes (d), and it is the lowest maturity level in the model (e). Quantitative management (b) is characteristic of Level 4, and continuous improvement (c) belongs to Level 5 (Optimizing).","topic":"t-cmm"},{"id":"A26-11-5","src":"A26","wk":11,"n":5,"type":"msq","text":"Which statements about the Defined (Level 3) maturity level are true?","opts":["Processes are primarily ad hoc","Processes are documented and standardized","A consistent process is followed across projects","Organizational standards are established","Continuous optimization is the primary focus"],"ans":[1,2,3],"exp":"At CMM Level 3 (Defined), processes are documented, standardized, and consistently followed across projects, with organizational standards established (b, c, d). Ad hoc processes (a) describe Level 1 (Initial), and continuous optimization (e) is the focus of Level 5 (Optimizing).","topic":"t-cmm"},{"id":"A26-11-6","src":"A26","wk":11,"n":6,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following correctly match a maturity level with its characteristic?","opts":["Repeatable (Level 2) – Disciplined process","Defined (Level 3) – Standard, consistent process","Initial (Level 1) – Continuously improving process","Managed (Level 4) – Predictable process","Optimizing (Level 5) – Ad hoc process"],"ans":[0,1,3],"exp":"The CMM maturity levels are correctly matched as follows: Repeatable (Level 2) focuses on disciplined project management processes, Defined (Level 3) establishes standardized and consistent processes across the organization, and Managed (Level 4) uses quantitative metrics to achieve predictable process performance. In contrast, Initial (Level 1) is characterized by ad hoc, chaotic processes rather than continuous improvement (which belongs to Level 5 Optimizing), and Optimizing (Level 5) is about continuous process improvement, not ad hoc behavior.","topic":"t-cmm"},{"id":"A26-11-7","src":"A26","wk":11,"n":7,"type":"msq","text":"Which statements about CMM Level 5 are correct?","opts":["It is less mature than the Defined level","It discourages process innovation","It focuses on optimization and continuous improvement","It includes Defect Prevention as a key process area","It includes Technology Change Management as a key process area"],"ans":[2,3,4],"exp":"CMM Level 5 (Optimizing) is the highest maturity level, focusing on continuous process improvement, defect prevention, and technology change management to proactively enhance quality and efficiency. It is more mature than the Defined level (Level 3), not less, and it actively encourages process innovation rather than discouraging it.","topic":"t-cmm"},{"id":"A26-11-8","src":"A26","wk":11,"n":8,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following are recognized Level 5 Key Process Areas (KPAs) in the SW-CMM?","opts":["Quantitative Process Management","Defect Prevention","Software Project Tracking and Oversight","Technology Change Management","Process Change Management"],"ans":[1,3,4],"exp":"In the SW-CMM, Level 5 (Optimizing) focuses on continuous process improvement through three Key Process Areas: Defect Prevention (to proactively eliminate errors), Technology Change Management (to adopt beneficial new technologies), and Process Change Management (to drive ongoing process enhancements). Quantitative Process Management belongs to Level 4 (Managed), and Software Project Tracking and Oversight is part of Level 2 (Repeatable), making (b), (d), and (e) the correct Level 5 KPAs.","topic":"t-cmm"},{"id":"A26-11-9","src":"A26","wk":11,"n":9,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements correctly describe Quality Assurance (QA)?","opts":["QA focuses on improving development processes","QA is limited to software testing","QA begins only after coding is completed","QA aims to prevent defects from occurring","QA is process-oriented"],"ans":[0,3,4],"exp":"Quality Assurance (QA) is a process-oriented approach that focuses on preventing defects by improving development and management processes throughout the lifecycle, starting from project inception. It is not limited to testing (which is Quality Control) and does not begin only after coding—it spans the entire development process.","topic":"t-quality"},{"id":"A26-11-10","src":"A26","wk":11,"n":10,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements correctly describe Quality Control (QC)?","opts":["QC aims primarily at process improvement","QC is performed only during requirements analysis","QC focuses on identifying defects in software products","QC is product-oriented","QC includes activities such as testing and inspections"],"ans":[2,3,4],"exp":"Quality Control (QC) is a product-oriented process that focuses on identifying defects in the actual work products through activities like testing, inspections, and reviews. It is performed throughout the development lifecycle—not just during requirements analysis—and its goal is defect detection, not process improvement (which is the aim of QA).","topic":"t-quality"},{"id":"A26-12-1","src":"A26","wk":12,"n":1,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following correctly match the CMMI flavor with its focus area?","opts":["CMMI for Development → Software and systems engineering activities","CMMI for Development → Exclusive focus on supplier contracts","CMMI for Services → Limited only to IT help desks","CMMI for Services → Organizations providing services","CMMI for Acquisition → Acquiring products and services"],"ans":[0,3,4],"exp":"CMMI has distinct models: CMMI for Development covers software, systems, and hardware engineering (a); CMMI for Services applies to any service-providing organization, not just IT help desks (d); and CMMI for Acquisition focuses on acquiring products and services (e). It does not exclusively focus on supplier contracts (b—that’s part of acquisition, not development), and it is not limited to IT help desks (c).","topic":"t-cmmi"},{"id":"A26-12-2","src":"A26","wk":12,"n":2,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements about the Personal Software Process (PSP) are true?","opts":["PSP recognizes that an individual process differs from a team project process","PSP provides a defined process for individual use","PSP is designed exclusively for large teams","PSP can be fine-tuned by individuals","PSP eliminates the need for any development process"],"ans":[0,1,3],"exp":"The Personal Software Process (PSP) is a disciplined framework designed for individual developers to plan, track, and improve their own work. It acknowledges the difference between individual and team processes (a), provides a defined personal process (b), and allows individuals to tailor it to their needs (d). It is not designed for large teams (c—that’s the Team Software Process, TSP), nor does it eliminate the need for other development processes (e).","topic":"t-cmmi"},{"id":"A26-12-3","src":"A26","wk":12,"n":3,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements about PSP evolution are true?","opts":["Measurement activities appear before planning activities","Quality management is introduced before personal planning","Personal process evolution is a PSP3 activity","Design and code reviews are introduced in PSP0","PSP consists of four levels: PSP0 through PSP3"],"ans":[0,2,4],"exp":"PSP evolves through four levels: PSP0 (basic measurement of time and defects), PSP1 (adds size estimation and planning), PSP2 (introduces design and code reviews for quality management), and PSP3 (focuses on personal process evolution and scaling). Thus, measurement (a) comes before planning, personal process evolution (c) is a PSP3 activity, and PSP has four levels (e). Quality management is introduced in PSP2, after planning (b), and reviews are not in PSP0 (d).","topic":"t-cmmi"},{"id":"A26-12-4","src":"A26","wk":12,"n":4,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements about Six Sigma are true?","opts":["Six Sigma allows up to 340 defects per million opportunities","A Six Sigma process produces no more than 3.4 defects per million opportunities","A Five Sigma process produces about 230 defects per million opportunities","A Four Sigma process produces about 6210 defects per million opportunities","Four Sigma produces fewer defects than Five Sigma"],"ans":[1,2,3],"exp":"Six Sigma is a quality management methodology that aims to reduce defects to 3.4 per million opportunities (DPMO), making (b) correct. A Five Sigma process corresponds to about 230 DPMO (c), and Four Sigma corresponds to about 6,210 DPMO (d). Option (a) is incorrect because 340 DPMO lies between Five Sigma and Six Sigma, not at Six Sigma. Option (e) is false because Four Sigma produces more defects (6,210) than Five Sigma (230).","topic":"t-cmmi"},{"id":"A26-12-5","src":"A26","wk":12,"n":5,"type":"msq","text":"Which statements explain why software reliability can vary among users?","opts":["Reliability is unaffected by how software is used","Users may exercise different features of the software","Different usage patterns can expose different failures","Every user interacts with software in exactly the same way","Failures visible to one user may not appear for another"],"ans":[1,2,4],"exp":"Software reliability can vary among users because different users may exercise different features (b), follow different usage patterns that expose distinct failures (c), and encounter failures that are visible to some but not others (e). Reliability is strongly affected by usage (a is false), and users do not interact identically (d is false).","topic":"t-reliab"},{"id":"A26-12-6","src":"A26","wk":12,"n":6,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements correctly compare software and hardware failure curves?","opts":["Software and hardware have identical failure behavior over time","Hardware typically exhibits a \"bathtub curve\" failure pattern","Software does not wear out physically with age like hardware","Hardware failure rate remains constant throughout its lifetime","Software changes can introduce new failures"],"ans":[1,2,4],"exp":"Hardware follows a \"bathtub curve\"—early failures, constant random failures during useful life, and increasing wear-out failures as it ages (b). Software, however, does not physically wear out (c); it can degrade due to changes, which may introduce new defects (e). Hardware failure rates are not constant throughout (d—they increase during burn-in and wear-out phases). Software and hardware do not have identical failure behavior (a).","topic":"t-reliab"},{"id":"A26-12-7","src":"A26","wk":12,"n":7,"type":"msq","text":"A POFOD value of 0.001 implies that:","opts":["Approximately 1 out of 1000 service requests may result in failure","Approximately 10 out of 1000 service requests may result in failure","The probability of failure on a demand is 0.1%","The system fails once every 1000 hours","The metric explicitly depends on time"],"ans":[0,2],"exp":"POFOD (Probability of Failure on Demand) measures the likelihood that a system will fail when a service is requested. A value of 0.001 means there is a 0.1% chance of failure per demand, which translates to approximately 1 failure out of 1,000 service requests (a and c). It does not imply 10 out of 1,000 (b—that would be 0.01), nor is it time-based (d and e—that would describe MTBF, not POFOD).","topic":"t-reliab"},{"id":"A26-12-8","src":"A26","wk":12,"n":8,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements about a reliability growth model are true?","opts":["It is used only after software deployment","It models how software reliability grows over time","Reliability improves as errors are detected and repaired","It assumes reliability decreases whenever defects are fixed","It can help estimate future reliability levels"],"ans":[1,2,4],"exp":"A reliability growth model tracks how software reliability improves over time as defects are detected and fixed, making (b) and (c) correct. It is primarily used during system testing, not only after deployment (a is false). It assumes reliability increases, not decreases, when defects are fixed (d is false). It can also help estimate future reliability levels (e).","topic":"t-relgrowth"},{"id":"A26-12-9","src":"A26","wk":12,"n":9,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following are implications of the Littlewood and Verall model?","opts":["Every bug contributes identically to reliability improvement","The order of bug removal has no effect on reliability growth","Reliability growth is not necessarily uniform across bug fixes","Early testing may produce substantial reliability gains","Remaining bugs after extensive testing may have smaller reliability impacts"],"ans":[2,3,4],"exp":"The Littlewood–Verrall model assumes that different faults have different sizes/impacts, so reliability growth is not uniform across fixes (c). Early testing tends to find the most impactful faults, leading to substantial early gains (d), while the remaining faults after extensive testing are likely to have smaller impacts (e). The model does not assume identical contributions (a), and the order of removal does affect growth because fixing larger-impact faults earlier yields more improvement (b is false).","topic":"t-relgrowth"},{"id":"A26-12-10","src":"A26","wk":12,"n":10,"type":"msq","text":"Which of the following statements about statistical testing are true?","opts":["Statistical testing uses only randomly generated source code","Test cases are selected based on the expected operational profile of the software","It ignores user behavior patterns","Statistical testing aims to reflect actual system usage","It can be used to assess software reliability"],"ans":[1,3,4],"exp":"Statistical testing (also called operational profile testing) selects test cases based on the expected operational profile (b) to reflect actual system usage (d). By measuring failures during such testing, it helps assess software reliability (e). It does not use randomly generated source code (a—it uses the actual software), and it explicitly incorporates user behavior patterns, not ignore them (c is false).","topic":"t-relgrowth"},{"id":"A19-1-1","src":"A19","wk":1,"n":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following can be considered to be a job rather than a project?","opts":["Construction of a house","A political election campaign","Buying grocery from a supermarket","A wedding"],"ans":[2],"exp":"A job is routine and repetitive with almost no uncertainty (buying groceries). House construction, a campaign and a wedding are one-off, goal-driven, time-bound: projects.","topic":"t-intro"},{"id":"A19-1-2","src":"A19","wk":1,"n":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Who among the following is not a project stakeholder?","opts":["Project manager","Customer","Project team member","Security personnel guarding the project building"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Stakeholders are people/organisations involved in, or affected by, the project (PM, customer, team, sponsor, top management, vendors). A security guard of the building is neither involved in nor affected by the project outcome.","topic":"t-intro"},{"id":"A19-1-3","src":"A19","wk":1,"n":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Managing a software development project is much harder than managing a road construction project. Which one of the following is NOT a major reason for this?","opts":["Large sized project","Managing something that is intangible","Managing intellectual work","Large impact of any requirements change"],"ans":[0],"exp":"Software is harder to manage because it is intangible, it is intellectual work and changes have a large ripple effect. Size is not special to software: a road project is also large.","topic":"t-intro"},{"id":"A19-1-4","src":"A19","wk":1,"n":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is not a characteristic of a software development project?","opts":["A software development project can have several non-routine tasks that involve challenge","A software development project can have several routine tasks","The resources available for a software development project are usually constrained","Every software development project has a well-defined starting point but may continue indefinitely"],"ans":[3],"exp":"A project is of finite duration with a defined start AND end. 'May continue indefinitely' describes an operation/job, not a project.","topic":"t-intro"},{"id":"A19-1-5","src":"A19","wk":1,"n":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following statements concerning the business case for a project is false?","opts":["It clearly states the cost that would be incurred to complete the project","It provides the benefits that are expected to accrue from undertaking the project","It presents a cost-benefit analysis","Once the business case is completed and approved by the project sponsor, the feasibility study is undertaken"],"ans":[3],"exp":"The order is the other way round: the feasibility study is what produces the business case (justification). So 'business case first, then feasibility study' is false.","topic":"t-bizcase"},{"id":"A19-1-6","src":"A19","wk":1,"n":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is the most acceptable definition of a project?","opts":["A series of activities","A set of activities undertaken within a defined time period in order to meet a specific set of goals/objectives within a budget","A coordinated effort to accomplish a well-defined goal but in general can continue indefinitely","Any activity that requires human and other resources for it to proceed"],"ans":[1],"exp":"PMBOK/lecture definition: a set of activities undertaken within a defined time period to meet a specific set of goals within a budget (temporary, unique, constrained).","topic":"t-intro"},{"id":"A19-1-7","src":"A19","wk":1,"n":7,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is usually not recognized as a phase of the software project management life cycle?","opts":["Execution","Initiation","Maintenance","Closing"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Project management life cycle: initiation → planning → execution (monitoring &amp; control) → closing. Maintenance belongs to the software (product) life cycle, not to project management.","topic":"t-pm"},{"id":"A19-1-8","src":"A19","wk":1,"n":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following most closely describes the sequence of phases of a project management life cycle?","opts":["Initiation, planning, executing, and closing","Concept, definition, development, closure","Initiation, definition, planning, monitoring","Concept, definition, implementation, maintenance"],"ans":[0],"exp":"The PMBOK-style sequence is initiation, planning, executing (with controlling) and closing.","topic":"t-pm"},{"id":"A19-1-9","src":"A19","wk":1,"n":9,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is not a pertinent question to ask for defining the scope of a project?","opts":["Why is the software being built?","What will be done?","When will it be done?","Who will be the project manager?"],"ans":[3],"exp":"W5HH asks Why, What, When, Who is responsible for a function, Where they are located, How (technically and managerially), How much. 'Who will be the project manager' is not a scope question.","topic":"t-intro"},{"id":"A19-1-10","src":"A19","wk":1,"n":10,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is not a software service type of project?","opts":["A software customization project","A novel antivirus software development project","A software maintenance project","A software testing project"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Services = customization, maintenance, testing, contract programming (work done on/for existing software at a customer's request). A novel antivirus is a new generic product.","topic":"t-intro"},{"id":"A19-2-1","src":"A19","wk":2,"n":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following SDLC models would be suitable for a moderate-sized project involving customization of a student registration and grading package for academic institutions? Assume the project would be developed by a team of experienced personnel and that the schedule has been very aggressively set.","opts":["Spiral model","Iterative waterfall model","V model","Agile model"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Customizing an existing package with an aggressive schedule and an experienced team is a service-type project with changing details: agile fits (frequent releases, small team).","topic":"t-agile"},{"id":"A19-2-2","src":"A19","wk":2,"n":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is not an agile software development methodology?","opts":["Extreme Programming (XP)","Scrum","Prototyping model","Lean software development"],"ans":[2],"exp":"XP, Scrum, Lean (and Crystal, DSDM, Unified Process) are agile methodologies. Prototyping is a waterfall-derived model.","topic":"t-agile"},{"id":"A19-2-3","src":"A19","wk":2,"n":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Members of a Scrum team are not expected to answer which one of the following questions during a daily Scrum meeting?","opts":["What did you do yesterday?","Why are you getting delayed?","What will you do today?","What obstacles are in your way?"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Daily scrum: What did you do yesterday? What will you do today? What obstacles are in your way? It is NOT a way to find out who is behind schedule or why.","topic":"t-agile"},{"id":"A19-2-4","src":"A19","wk":2,"n":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is not a Sprint ceremony in Scrum?","opts":["Sprint planning meeting","Daily stand-up meeting","Sprint retrospective meeting","Project start-up meeting"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Scrum ceremonies: sprint planning, daily scrum (stand-up), sprint review, sprint retrospective. There is no 'project start-up meeting' ceremony.","topic":"t-agile"},{"id":"A19-2-5","src":"A19","wk":2,"n":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following characteristics of a moderate-sized project would indicate the suitability of the Extreme Programming (XP) model in preference to the waterfall model?","opts":["Stable and well understood requirements","Mission critical software","Service-oriented project","Extremely large project"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Agile/XP suits service-type projects (customization, maintenance) with changing requirements and small teams. Stable requirements, mission-critical or very large projects point to waterfall/V-model.","topic":"t-agile"},{"id":"A19-2-6","src":"A19","wk":2,"n":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following life cycle models is least similar to the waterfall model?","opts":["Prototyping model","V model","Spiral model","Scrum"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Prototyping, V and spiral all keep the step-wise waterfall phases (spiral subsumes waterfall). Scrum is an agile, iterative framework with no phase sequence: least similar.","topic":"t-agile"},{"id":"A19-2-7","src":"A19","wk":2,"n":7,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is not a goal of requirements analysis?","opts":["Weed out ambiguities in the requirements","Weed out inconsistencies in the requirements","Weed out non-functional requirements","Weed out incompleteness in the requirements"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Requirements analysis removes ambiguity, inconsistency and incompleteness. Non-functional requirements are part of the SRS; they are documented, not 'weeded out'.","topic":"t-waterfall"},{"id":"A19-2-8","src":"A19","wk":2,"n":8,"type":"mcq","text":"How is agility achieved in the agile model of software development?","opts":["Elaborate documentation","Rigorous planning","Avoidance of things that waste time","Prototype construction"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Agility is achieved by fitting the process to the project and avoiding things that waste time (heavy documentation, long-term plans).","topic":"t-agile"},{"id":"A19-2-9","src":"A19","wk":2,"n":9,"type":"mcq","text":"In the agile model, why are spikes constructed during development?","opts":["To explore potential solutions","To propose a common vision of what is required","To restructure code without affecting behaviour","To construct simpler use cases"],"ans":[0],"exp":"Spike = a simple program to explore potential solutions. Metaphor = common vision; refactoring = restructure without changing behaviour; user stories are simpler than use cases.","topic":"t-agile"},{"id":"A19-2-10","src":"A19","wk":2,"n":10,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is false about an iteration in the agile model?","opts":["A new release is made at the end of each iteration","A new release may not be made at the end of some iterations","No new functionality may have been incorporated at the end of an iteration","Existing functionalities may have been enhanced at the end of an iteration"],"ans":[1],"exp":"In agile a new release is invariably made at the end of every iteration, even if little functionality was added. So 'a release may not be made' is false.","topic":"t-agile"},{"id":"A19-3-1","src":"A19","wk":3,"n":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is not an element of project portfolio management?","opts":["Portfolio definition","Portfolio management","Portfolio optimization","Portfolio deletion"],"ans":[3],"exp":"The three PPM elements are portfolio definition, portfolio management and portfolio optimisation. There is no 'portfolio deletion'.","topic":"t-bizcase"},{"id":"A19-3-2","src":"A19","wk":3,"n":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is the proper sequence of undertaking project portfolio management activities?","opts":["Portfolio definition, portfolio optimization, portfolio management","Portfolio optimization, portfolio definition, portfolio management","Portfolio definition, portfolio management, portfolio optimization","Portfolio management, portfolio definition, portfolio optimization"],"ans":[2],"exp":"First define the portfolio (what projects are in it), then manage it (record costs/performance), then optimise it (balance risk vs value).","topic":"t-bizcase"},{"id":"A19-3-3","src":"A19","wk":3,"n":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Consider that a software development department of a company is being set up. Which one of the following can be considered to be a direct cost?","opts":["Insurance","Building maintenance","Purchase of a box of CDs","Air conditioning"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Direct costs can be associated with a specific money figure/item (a box of CDs). Insurance, building maintenance and air-conditioning are overheads (indirect).","topic":"t-bizcase"},{"id":"A19-3-4","src":"A19","wk":3,"n":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Consider a company that has undertaken a software development project. Which of the following is not a tangible benefit?","opts":["Benefits due to bonus payment on completing the project in fewer hours","Benefits due to an improved corporate image","Benefits due to producing reports with no errors","Benefits due to saving manpower costs"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Tangible benefits can be measured (fewer hours, error-free reports, manpower savings). An improved corporate image is real but not measurable: intangible.","topic":"t-bizcase"},{"id":"A19-3-5","src":"A19","wk":3,"n":5,"type":"mcq","text":"The method of IRR is considered to be inferior to the NPV method, because","opts":["it may give multiple rates of return","it is relatively difficult to compute","it does not indicate the absolute size of the return","All of the above"],"ans":[3],"exp":"IRR can give multiple rates, is harder to compute, and does not show the absolute size of the return (NPV 100k at 15 % may beat NPV 10k at 18 %). Hence all of the above.","topic":"t-cba"},{"id":"A19-3-6","src":"A19","wk":3,"n":6,"type":"mcq","text":"In break-even analysis, the investment period is the period when","opts":["the cost of the candidate system is less than that of the current system","the cost of the candidate system is higher than that of the current system","the cost of the candidate system is equal to that of the current system","none of the above"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Break-even analysis compares current vs candidate system costs. Before break-even the candidate costs more: investment period. After it, the candidate gives more benefit: return period.","topic":"t-cba"},{"id":"A19-3-7","src":"A19","wk":3,"n":7,"type":"mcq","text":"Where a project is relatively risky, it is a common practice to use","opts":["a lower discount rate to calculate net present value","an average discount rate to calculate net present value","a higher discount rate to calculate net present value","none of the above"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Risky projects are discounted with a higher rate (a risk premium of e.g. +2 % for a safe project, +5 % for a fairly risky one).","topic":"t-riskeval"},{"id":"A19-3-8","src":"A19","wk":3,"n":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following risk evaluation methods is suitable when it is required to evaluate whether the risk is important and decide on a suitable course of action?","opts":["Risk identification and ranking","C-B analysis","Risk profile analysis","Using decision trees"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Decision trees are used when the manager must decide a course of action (e.g. extend vs replace a system) and evaluate expected values of each path.","topic":"t-riskeval"},{"id":"A19-3-9","src":"A19","wk":3,"n":9,"type":"mcq","text":"The projects that an organization undertakes within a planning cycle are referred to as","opts":["Business cycle programmes","Strategic programmes","Infrastructure programmes","Research and development programmes"],"ans":[0],"exp":"Business cycle programmes = the projects an organisation undertakes within a particular planning cycle (e.g. the year's budget).","topic":"t-programme"},{"id":"A19-3-10","src":"A19","wk":3,"n":10,"type":"mcq","text":"Benefits that BSNL will get from obtaining local administration approval for constructing a mobile tower in a street can be","opts":["quantified and valued","quantified but not valued","identified and easily quantified","identified but not easily quantified"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Public approval/goodwill of the locality is a benefit you can identify but cannot easily quantify (the lecture's example: public approval for an organisation in its locality).","topic":"t-programme"},{"id":"A19-4-1","src":"A19","wk":4,"n":1,"type":"mcq","text":"How many man-hours is a man-month?","opts":["142","152","162","172"],"ans":[1],"exp":"152 hours per person-month = 19 working days × 8 hours (COCOMO default).","topic":"t-estbasics"},{"id":"A19-4-2","src":"A19","wk":4,"n":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is the correct order in which a software project manager estimates various project parameters while using COCOMO?","opts":["cost, effort, duration, size","cost, duration, effort, size","size, effort, duration, cost","size, cost, effort, duration"],"ans":[2],"exp":"COCOMO starts from size (KLOC), computes effort (PM), then duration (Tdev = f(effort)), then cost (effort × rate).","topic":"t-estbasics"},{"id":"A19-4-3","src":"A19","wk":4,"n":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is NOT a factor for 'lines of code' being considered a poor software size metric?","opts":["It is programming language dependent","It penalizes efficient and compact coding","It is programmer dependent","It is dependent on the complexity of the requirements"],"ans":[3],"exp":"LOC is criticised for being language-dependent, programmer-dependent (style) and for penalising compact code. Depending on requirement complexity is what a size measure SHOULD do.","topic":"t-esttech"},{"id":"A19-4-4","src":"A19","wk":4,"n":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following estimating methods is most appropriate when detailed information about the project is lacking?","opts":["Parametric models","Basic expert judgement technique","Analogy-based method","Price to win"],"ans":[3],"exp":"When detailed information is lacking, price-to-win (cost = what the customer can spend, then constrain development to that) may be the only appropriate strategy.","topic":"t-esttech"},{"id":"A19-4-5","src":"A19","wk":4,"n":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is NOT a limitation of bottom-up estimation?","opts":["It may overlook many of the system-level costs","It may be inaccurate because the necessary information may not be available in the early phase","It tends to underestimate and overlook complexities of low-level components","It tends to be more time-consuming"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Underestimating low-level complexity is a weakness of TOP-DOWN estimation. Bottom-up's weaknesses: overlooks system-level costs, needs early detail, time-consuming.","topic":"t-esttech"},{"id":"A19-4-6","src":"A19","wk":4,"n":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is NOT a desirable characteristic of a good algorithmic estimation model?","opts":["A clear procedure for estimation is provided","Subjective factors are avoided","Results are valid for a wide range of parameter values","Requires data that are accurately known only in future"],"ans":[3],"exp":"A good algorithmic model is causal: it needs only data available now, not data known accurately only in future.","topic":"t-esttech"},{"id":"A19-4-7","src":"A19","wk":4,"n":7,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is NOT a key component in function point analysis?","opts":["External Inputs","External Outputs","External Operations","External Inquiries"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Five IFPUG components: external inputs, external outputs, external inquiries, internal logical files, external interface files. 'External operations' is not one.","topic":"t-fp"},{"id":"A19-4-8","src":"A19","wk":4,"n":8,"type":"mcq","text":"In function point analysis, how many general system characteristics are evaluated and used to compute the Value Adjustment Factor (VAF)?","opts":["12","13","14","15"],"ans":[2],"exp":"14 general system characteristics, each rated 0-5; TDI = sum; VAF = 0.65 + 0.01 × TDI (range 0.65 to 1.35).","topic":"t-fp"},{"id":"A19-4-9","src":"A19","wk":4,"n":9,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is NOT a disadvantage of function point analysis?","opts":["It is labour intensive","Extensive training is required","It does not consider algorithmic complexity of a function","It is hard to fudge"],"ans":[3],"exp":"'Hard to fudge' is a PRO of function points. Cons: labour-intensive, extensive training, ignores algorithmic complexity, inexperience gives inaccuracy.","topic":"t-fp"},{"id":"A19-4-10","src":"A19","wk":4,"n":10,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following function points is most suitable for use in embedded system development projects?","opts":["IFPUG function points","Mark II function points","COSMIC function points","None of the above"],"ans":[2],"exp":"COSMIC full function points were designed for embedded/real-time software (layers, data movements: entries, exits, reads, writes). IFPUG and Mark II target information systems.","topic":"t-fp"},{"id":"A19-5-1","src":"A19","wk":5,"n":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is an example of an organic project?","opts":["An accounting system development project","A compiler writing project","An operating system development project","A real-time database system development project"],"ans":[0],"exp":"Organic = familiar, stable, small team, information systems (accounting, student info). Compilers/linkers = semi-detached; OS, real-time systems = embedded.","topic":"t-cocomo"},{"id":"A19-5-2","src":"A19","wk":5,"n":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following statements is true of the COCOMO model?","opts":["Cost is considered to be the most fundamental attribute of a software product, based on which the project size and duration are measured","Size is considered to be the most fundamental attribute of a software product, based on which the project cost and duration are measured","Effort is considered to be the most fundamental attribute of a software product, based on which the project size and cost are measured","Duration is considered to be the most fundamental attribute of a software product, based on which the project size and effort are measured"],"ans":[1],"exp":"In COCOMO size is the fundamental attribute: effort, duration and cost are all derived from size.","topic":"t-cocomo"},{"id":"A19-5-3","src":"A19","wk":5,"n":3,"type":"mcq","text":"In intermediate COCOMO, if modern programming practices are used, then the initial cost and effort estimates obtained by the basic COCOMO are","opts":["scaled downwards","scaled upwards","remained unchanged","none of the above"],"ans":[0],"exp":"MODP (modern programming practices) multiplier is below 1 when practices are used (High 0.91, Very high 0.82): estimates scale downwards.","topic":"t-cocomo"},{"id":"A19-5-4","src":"A19","wk":5,"n":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following COCOMO estimation models considers the project as a collection of heterogeneous sub-systems?","opts":["Basic COCOMO","Intermediate COCOMO","Detailed (Complete) COCOMO","All of the above"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Complete/Detailed COCOMO treats the system as heterogeneous sub-systems (e.g. database semi-detached, GUI organic, communication embedded) and adds their costs.","topic":"t-cocomo"},{"id":"A19-5-5","src":"A19","wk":5,"n":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following COCOMO II estimation levels would provide the most accurate estimations?","opts":["Application composition","Early design","Post-architecture","All COCOMO II estimation levels provide equally accurate results"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Accuracy increases as more is known: application composition → early design → post-architecture (17 multipliers, most detail).","topic":"t-cocomo2"},{"id":"A19-5-6","src":"A19","wk":5,"n":6,"type":"mcq","text":"The early design model of COCOMO II would be used","opts":["when software is composed from existing parts","when requirements are available but design has not yet started","to compute the effort of integrating reusable components","once the system architecture has been designed and more information about the system is available"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Early design: requirements available, design not yet started (7 effort multipliers). Post-architecture: after architecture design.","topic":"t-cocomo2"},{"id":"A19-5-7","src":"A19","wk":5,"n":7,"type":"mcq","text":"In COCOMO II, the scale factor Architecture/risk resolution (RESL) refers to","opts":["The degree to which there are past examples that can be consulted","The degree of flexibility that exists when implementing the project","The degree of uncertainty about requirements","None of the above"],"ans":[2],"exp":"RESL (architecture/risk resolution) reflects the degree of uncertainty about requirements. PREC = past examples; FLEX = flexibility in implementing.","topic":"t-cocomo2"},{"id":"A19-5-8","src":"A19","wk":5,"n":8,"type":"mcq","text":"In Halstead's software science, the higher the level of a language,","opts":["the less effort it takes to develop a program using that language","the more effort it takes to develop a program using that language","the same effort it takes to develop a program using that language","None of the above"],"ans":[0],"exp":"Program level L = V*/V; effort E = V/L. A higher-level language has a higher L, so E is smaller: less effort (assembly takes more effort than a high-level language).","topic":"t-halstead"},{"id":"A19-5-9","src":"A19","wk":5,"n":9,"type":"mcq","text":"Suppose the development time of a large software product has been estimated to be one and a half years. Then, in order to develop the product in nine months, what will be the total increase in development effort? (Use Putnam's model.)","opts":["2 times","4 times","8 times","16 times"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Putnam: K ∝ 1/td⁴. 18 → 9 months halves td, so effort grows (18/9)⁴ = 16 times.","topic":"t-staffing"},{"id":"A19-5-10","src":"A19","wk":5,"n":10,"type":"mcq","text":"Boehm observed: \"There is a limit beyond which the schedule of a software project cannot be reduced by buying any more personnel or equipment.\" This limit occurs roughly at","opts":["25% of the nominal time estimate","50% of the nominal time estimate","75% of the nominal time estimate","90% of the nominal time estimate"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Boehm: schedule cannot be compressed below about 75 % of the nominal estimate by adding people or equipment (limited parallel activities).","topic":"t-staffing"},{"id":"A19-6-1","src":"A19","wk":6,"n":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following charts would be the most useful to decompose the project activities into smaller tasks that are more meaningfully managed?","opts":["PERT chart","Gantt chart","Task network representation","Work breakdown structure"],"ans":[3],"exp":"A work breakdown structure decomposes the project hierarchically into smaller, manageable tasks. It shows neither durations nor precedence.","topic":"t-wbs"},{"id":"A19-6-2","src":"A19","wk":6,"n":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is the critical path for the activity network given in the figure? (Numbers on the arrows are durations.)","opts":["A, B, F, H, G, I, L","A, B, F, H, I, L","A, C, D, J, K, L","A, C, D, G, K, L"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Path lengths: A-C-D-J-K-L = 2+3+3+1+3 = 12 (longest). A-C-D-G-K-L = 11, A-B-E-H-I-L = 11, A-B-E-L = 10, A-B-F-H-I-L = 8. Critical path = longest.","topic":"t-cpm","fig":"net19"},{"id":"A19-6-3","src":"A19","wk":6,"n":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Consider the portion of a PERT network shown in the figure. What is the LF of activity C?","opts":["10","11","16","17"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Backward pass at a burst point: LF of C = minimum of the LS of its successors = min(LS of D = 17, LS of E = 17) = 17.","topic":"t-cpm","fig":"cnode19"},{"id":"A19-6-4","src":"A19","wk":6,"n":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Consider the portion of a PERT network shown in the figure. What is the ES of activity C?","opts":["9","10","11","12"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Forward pass at a merge point: ES of C = maximum of the EF of its predecessors = max(EF of A = 9, EF of B = 10) = 10.","topic":"t-cpm","fig":"cnode19"},{"id":"A19-6-5","src":"A19","wk":6,"n":5,"type":"mcq","text":"The PERT method differs from CPM in which one of the following aspects?","opts":["PERT uses statistical time durations whereas CPM uses deterministic time durations","PERT uses dummy activities whereas CPM does not","PERT uses free float, whereas CPM uses total float in critical path calculations","PERT uses activity on arc whereas CPM uses activity on node networks"],"ans":[0],"exp":"PERT uses three time estimates (statistical: te = (a+4m+b)/6), CPM uses one deterministic duration per activity.","topic":"t-cpm"},{"id":"A19-6-6","src":"A19","wk":6,"n":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Activities, durations and predecessors: A = 14 (–), B = 13 (A), C = 20 (A), D = 10 (B, C). What is the total project duration?","opts":["37","44","47","57"],"ans":[1],"exp":"A (0-14), B (14-27), C (14-34), D starts after both B and C: max(27, 34) = 34, finishes 34+10 = 44.","topic":"t-cpm"},{"id":"A19-6-7","src":"A19","wk":6,"n":7,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is true of a critical path in a PERT chart?","opts":["It is the path having the maximum number of tasks","It is the shortest path in terms of time","It is the longest path in terms of time","It is the path with the largest amount of slack time"],"ans":[2],"exp":"The critical path is the longest path (in time) from start to finish; it fixes the minimum project duration. It need not have the most tasks.","topic":"t-cpm"},{"id":"A19-6-8","src":"A19","wk":6,"n":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following statements regarding critical paths in a PERT chart is true?","opts":["A critical path through a PERT chart is any path that contains the least number of edges","Some activities on the critical path can have slack","Every PERT chart has exactly one critical path","It is possible that in the PERT chart for a project, there can be multiple critical paths, all having exactly the same duration"],"ans":[3],"exp":"A network can have several critical paths of exactly equal duration. Critical activities have zero slack.","topic":"t-cpm"},{"id":"A19-6-9","src":"A19","wk":6,"n":9,"type":"mcq","text":"Activity (immediate followers, time in weeks): A (E, B; 3), B (C, F; 2), C (D, G; 8), D (F; 4), E (F; 5), F (–; 10), G (–; 10). Which sequence of activities is on the critical path?","opts":["A-E-F","A-B-C-G","A-B-C-D-F","A-B-F"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Paths: A-E-F = 3+5+10 = 18, A-B-C-G = 3+2+8+10 = 23, A-B-C-D-F = 3+2+8+4+10 = 27, A-B-F = 3+2+10 = 15. Longest = A-B-C-D-F.","topic":"t-cpm"},{"id":"A19-6-10","src":"A19","wk":6,"n":10,"type":"mcq","text":"Same project as before but E now takes 3 weeks: A (E, B; 3), B (C, F; 2), C (D, G; 8), D (F; 4), E (F; 3), F (–; 10), G (–; 10). Which one of the following paths has the greatest slack time?","opts":["A-E-F","A-B-C-G","A-B-C-D-F","A-B-F"],"ans":[3],"exp":"With E = 3: A-E-F = 16, A-B-C-G = 23, A-B-C-D-F = 27, A-B-F = 15. Slack = 27 − path length, largest for the shortest path A-B-F (12).","topic":"t-cpm"},{"id":"A19-7-1","src":"A19","wk":7,"n":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following may be the highest motivator for a senior manager of a software development organization?","opts":["Lunch allowance","House rent allowance","Company provided car","More interesting job assignment at the same pay"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Senior managers have their lower needs met; per Maslow/Herzberg the motivator is the job content itself (interesting, challenging work), not allowances.","topic":"t-people"},{"id":"A19-7-2","src":"A19","wk":7,"n":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is the most accurate ranking of human needs as per Maslow (highest first)?","opts":["Self actualization; self promotion; social; security and physiological needs","Self esteem; self actualization; social; security and physiological","Self actualization; self esteem; social; security and physiological","Self fulfillment; self esteem; social; security and physiological"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Maslow, top to bottom: self-actualisation, esteem, social (love/belonging), safety/security, physiological.","topic":"t-people"},{"id":"A19-7-3","src":"A19","wk":7,"n":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following has been identified by Herzberg as a factor which, if present, will lead to increased motivation?","opts":["Good supervision","Job security","Regular promotions","Documented company policies"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Herzberg motivators: recognition, the job itself, achievement, opportunity for promotion. Supervision, security and company policy are hygiene factors.","topic":"t-people"},{"id":"A19-7-4","src":"A19","wk":7,"n":4,"type":"mcq","text":"If a team member is finding it extremely stressful, which one of the following stress management techniques would be suggested by a systemic approach?","opts":["Rolling head from side to side","Change of job role","Deep breathing","Increased social interactions"],"ans":[1],"exp":"A systemic (organisational) approach removes the cause of stress, e.g. changing the job role, rather than individual relaxation techniques.","topic":"t-people"},{"id":"A19-7-5","src":"A19","wk":7,"n":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one is not a factor in job satisfaction according to the Oldham-Hackman job characteristics model?","opts":["Skill variety","Task identity","Task complexity","Task significance"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Oldham-Hackman: skill variety, task identity, task significance (+ autonomy, feedback). Task complexity is not one of them.","topic":"t-people"},{"id":"A19-7-6","src":"A19","wk":7,"n":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is a 'hygiene factor' under Herzberg's Motivation-Hygiene theory?","opts":["Recognition for excellent work","Self-actualization","Good relations with co-workers and managers","Clean work environment"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Hygiene factors relate to the job context: pay, working conditions, company policy, supervision and relations with co-workers. Recognition and self-actualisation are motivators. Note: a clean work environment (working conditions) is ALSO a hygiene factor in Herzberg's theory, but the 2019 key accepted only (c). If both appear in an MSQ, tick both.","topic":"t-people"},{"id":"A19-7-7","src":"A19","wk":7,"n":7,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following types of team organization would be most suitable for a small yet complex project?","opts":["Chief programmer","Democratic","Hybrid","Squat"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Democratic (egoless) teams suit small but complex/research-type projects: everyone reviews, ideas are pooled. Chief programmer teams suit simple, well-understood work.","topic":"t-org"},{"id":"A19-7-8","src":"A19","wk":7,"n":8,"type":"mcq","text":"In which one of the following types of organization is team building likely to be most difficult?","opts":["Functional","Projectized","Matrix","Hybrid"],"ans":[2],"exp":"In a matrix organisation members report to two bosses (functional and project) and shift between projects, so building a stable team is hardest.","topic":"t-org"},{"id":"A19-7-9","src":"A19","wk":7,"n":9,"type":"mcq","text":"Joy works for a company that accepts outsourcing projects from overseas clients. Each time it accepts a project it sets up a team; when the project completes the team is dissolved and the engineers are assigned to other projects. What is the organization structure of Joy's company?","opts":["Matrix","Projectized","Functional","Hybrid"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Team formed per project and dissolved at the end, members belong to the project for its duration: projectized (project) organisation.","topic":"t-org"},{"id":"A19-7-10","src":"A19","wk":7,"n":10,"type":"mcq","text":"A hardware vendor has told you that a customs-clearance problem may delay your network equipment by several months. You arrange to lease network equipment from a local company as an interim arrangement. Which risk response strategy have you adopted?","opts":["Transference","Acceptance","Mitigation","Avoidance"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Leasing interim equipment reduces the impact of the delay without removing the risk: mitigation. Transference would shift it to a third party (insurance/contract); avoidance would remove the cause.","topic":"t-risk"},{"id":"A19-8-1","src":"A19","wk":8,"n":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is true regarding the activity schedule?","opts":["It indicates the planned start and completion dates for each activity","It shows the dates on which each resource will be required and the level of requirement","It shows the planned cumulative expenditure incurred by the use of resources over time","All of the above"],"ans":[0],"exp":"Activity schedule = planned start and completion dates of each activity. Resource schedule = when each resource is needed and at what level. Cost schedule = planned cumulative expenditure.","topic":"t-resource"},{"id":"A19-8-2","src":"A19","wk":8,"n":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following shows the dates on which each resource will be required for an activity and the level of that requirement?","opts":["Activity schedule","Resource schedule","Cost schedule","All of the above"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Resource schedule: dates on which each resource will be required and the level of that requirement.","topic":"t-resource"},{"id":"A19-8-3","src":"A19","wk":8,"n":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is FALSE regarding resource scheduling?","opts":["Resource scheduling may lead to changing the durations of some activities on the PERT chart","Resource scheduling may not affect the critical path","Resource scheduling usually shortens the critical path","Resource scheduling can create additional critical paths"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Resource scheduling usually DELAYS activities (uses float) and may create new critical paths; it does not shorten the critical path.","topic":"t-resource"},{"id":"A19-8-4","src":"A19","wk":8,"n":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Given: 1. shortest critical activities, 2. critical activities, 3. non-critical activities, 4. non-critical activities with least float, 5. shortest non-critical activities. Which is the correct order of priorities in Burman's priority list?","opts":["1-2-3-4-5","1-2-5-4-3","2-1-5-4-3","5-4-3-2-1"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Burman's priority list: (1) shortest critical activities, (2) other critical activities, (3) shortest non-critical, (4) non-critical with least float, (5) other non-critical.","topic":"t-resource"},{"id":"A19-8-5","src":"A19","wk":8,"n":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is the best way of publishing and communicating project schedules?","opts":["Activity plan","Activity bar charts","Resource histograms","Work schedule"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Activity plans, bar charts and histograms are planning tools; a work plan/work schedule (lists or charts per person) is the best way to publish and communicate schedules.","topic":"t-costsched"},{"id":"A19-8-6","src":"A19","wk":8,"n":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is the proper scheduling sequence?","opts":["activity plan, resource allocation, risk assessment, cost schedule","activity plan, risk assessment, cost schedule, resource allocation","activity plan, risk assessment, resource allocation, cost schedule","activity plan, cost schedule, risk assessment, resource allocation"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Activity plan → risk assessment → resource allocation → cost schedule (each later step may force revisions of the earlier ones).","topic":"t-costsched"},{"id":"A19-8-7","src":"A19","wk":8,"n":7,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is an example of the oral formal regular reporting method?","opts":["Weekly progress meetings","End-of-stage review meetings","Progress reports","Exception reports"],"ans":[0],"exp":"Oral + formal + regular = weekly/monthly progress meetings. End-of-stage reviews are oral formal ad hoc; progress reports written formal regular; exception reports written formal ad hoc.","topic":"t-monitor"},{"id":"A19-8-8","src":"A19","wk":8,"n":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following checkpoints is a time-driven checkpoint?","opts":["Production of the SRS document","Production of a coding module","Production of test cases","Checking the progress monthly"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Time-driven checkpoints fall on pre-set dates (weekly/monthly checks). Event-driven checkpoints are tied to deliverables (SRS, module, test cases).","topic":"t-monitor"},{"id":"A19-8-9","src":"A19","wk":8,"n":9,"type":"mcq","text":"In Red/Amber/Green reporting, Amber represents which one of the following?","opts":["The tasks are 'on target'","The tasks are 'not on target but recoverable'","The tasks are 'not on target and recoverable only with difficulty'","The tasks are 'not on target and not recoverable'"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Green = on target; Amber = not on target but recoverable; Red = not on target and recoverable only with difficulty.","topic":"t-monitor"},{"id":"A19-8-10","src":"A19","wk":8,"n":10,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following statements concerning reviews is true?","opts":["Usually, review is more cost-effective in removing defects as compared to testing","Usually, review is less cost-effective in removing defects as compared to testing","Usually, review is as cost-effective in removing defects as testing","Reviews are considered a dynamic analysis technique"],"ans":[0],"exp":"Reviews find defects directly (no debugging needed) and work on non-executable documents too, so they are more cost-effective than testing. Reviews are static, not dynamic.","topic":"t-review"},{"id":"A19-9-1","src":"A19","wk":9,"n":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Code review does not target to detect which one of the following types of errors?","opts":["Logical error","Algorithmic error","Syntax error","Programming errors"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Code review is done after a module compiles, so syntax errors are already gone. It targets logical, algorithmic and programming errors.","topic":"t-codereview"},{"id":"A19-9-2","src":"A19","wk":9,"n":2,"type":"mcq","text":"When is code review performed during the software life cycle?","opts":["After unit testing","After coding and compiling","During integration testing","During system testing"],"ans":[1],"exp":"After the module is coded and successfully compiled (syntax-clean), before testing.","topic":"t-codereview"},{"id":"A19-9-3","src":"A19","wk":9,"n":3,"type":"mcq","text":"The Cleanroom technique does not use which one of the following activities?","opts":["Code walkthrough","Code inspection","Formal verification for bug removal","Testing of code by executing it"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Cleanroom relies on walkthroughs, inspection and formal verification; programmers may not test their code by executing it (only syntax checks with a compiler).","topic":"t-codereview"},{"id":"A19-9-4","src":"A19","wk":9,"n":4,"type":"mcq","text":"In a slip chart, the more the slip line bends,","opts":["the greater is the variation from the plan","the smaller is the variation from the plan","no change happens in the plan","None of the above"],"ans":[0],"exp":"The more a slip line bends (jagged), the greater the deviation from plan; a very jagged line means rescheduling is needed.","topic":"t-visual"},{"id":"A19-9-5","src":"A19","wk":9,"n":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following charts shows the slippage of the project completion date through the life of the project?","opts":["Gantt chart","Slip chart","Timeline chart","PERT chart"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Gantt and slip charts are snapshots. The timeline chart records how target completion dates changed over the life of the project.","topic":"t-visual"},{"id":"A19-9-6","src":"A19","wk":9,"n":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following methods is preferred for assigning an earned value (EV) in software projects?","opts":["The 0/100 technique","The 50/50 technique","The 75/25 technique","The milestone technique"],"ans":[0],"exp":"The 0/100 technique (value only when finished) is preferred for software because partial completion is hard to judge (99 % complete syndrome).","topic":"t-eva"},{"id":"A19-9-7","src":"A19","wk":9,"n":7,"type":"mcq","text":"Work with a PV of Rs. 40,000 should have been completed by now. Some of that work has not been done, so the EV is only Rs. 30,000. What is the Schedule Variance (SV)?","opts":["10000","-10000","70000","None of the above"],"ans":[1],"exp":"SV = EV − PV = 30,000 − 40,000 = −10,000 (negative ⇒ behind schedule).","topic":"t-eva"},{"id":"A19-9-8","src":"A19","wk":9,"n":8,"type":"mcq","text":"The cost performance index (CPI) is given by which of the following expressions?","opts":["CPI = AC/EV","CPI = EV/AC","CPI = AC × EV","CPI = EV − AC"],"ans":[1],"exp":"CPI = EV / AC (value earned per rupee spent; &lt; 1 means over cost).","topic":"t-eva"},{"id":"A19-9-9","src":"A19","wk":9,"n":9,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following would equal EV/PV?","opts":["SPI","CPI","SV","CV"],"ans":[0],"exp":"SPI = EV / PV.","topic":"t-eva"},{"id":"A19-9-10","src":"A19","wk":9,"n":10,"type":"mcq","text":"Assume that a medium-sized project is experiencing considerable schedule slippage. Which one of the following may not help to get the project back on schedule?","opts":["Shortening the critical path","Altering the activity precedence requirements","Speeding up non-critical path activities","Reducing scope of the project"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Project duration is set by the critical path; speeding up activities that already have float does not bring the end date forward.","topic":"t-backontrack"},{"id":"A19-10-1","src":"A19","wk":10,"n":1,"type":"mcq","text":"In software configuration management, system accounting deals with","opts":["Keeping track of who made a particular change to a configuration item, what change was exactly made, and when the change was made","Controlling access to the different deliverable objects","Undoing changes to some part of a revision","Preventing unauthorized changes to the work products"],"ans":[0],"exp":"System accounting = keeping track of who changed which configuration item, what exactly changed, and when.","topic":"t-scm"},{"id":"A19-10-2","src":"A19","wk":10,"n":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following scenarios can occur even while a software development organization is impeccably using a configuration management tool?","opts":["Two team members overwrite each other's code that is under configuration control","Accidental deletion of a work product that is under configuration control and consequent loss of work","A user reporting a software configuration error during installation","Unauthorized modifications to a work product under configuration control by one of the developers"],"ans":[2],"exp":"A CM tool prevents overwrites, losses and unauthorised changes inside the development environment. It cannot stop a user from mis-configuring the product during installation.","topic":"t-scm"},{"id":"A19-10-3","src":"A19","wk":10,"n":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Pre-controlled work products are","opts":["those that are put under configuration control","those that are not yet under configuration control, but will eventually be under configuration control","those that will not be subject to configuration control","none of the above"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Controlled = under configuration control; pre-controlled = not yet, but will be; uncontrolled = never under configuration control.","topic":"t-scm"},{"id":"A19-10-4","src":"A19","wk":10,"n":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following statements is NOT correct regarding the SCCS tool?","opts":["It is a popular configuration management tool on UNIX systems","It can be used for controlling and managing different versions of text files","It can handle binary files","It provides an efficient way of storing versions that minimizes the amount of occupied disk space"],"ans":[2],"exp":"SCCS and RCS manage TEXT files using deltas; they do not handle binary files (executables, documents with diagrams).","topic":"t-scm"},{"id":"A19-10-5","src":"A19","wk":10,"n":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which is true concerning a bespoke system?","opts":["It is a system which is created especially for one customer","It is a package which is bought 'as is'","It is a core system which is customized to meet the needs of the client","none of the above"],"ans":[0],"exp":"Bespoke = created specially for one customer. Off-the-shelf = bought as is. COTS = core system customised for the client.","topic":"t-contract"},{"id":"A19-10-6","src":"A19","wk":10,"n":6,"type":"mcq","text":"The contract in which the customer is charged at a fixed rate per unit of effort is known as","opts":["fixed price contract","time and materials contract","fixed price per unit delivered contract","none of the above"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Time and materials: the customer pays a fixed rate per unit of effort (per staff-hour or person-month).","topic":"t-contract"},{"id":"A19-10-7","src":"A19","wk":10,"n":7,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is typically not expected in a fixed price contract?","opts":["Vendors tend to overprice the bids","The selected vendor may demand a higher price to accommodate new requirements","Better quality of software is realized compared to time and material contracts","Best model to follow when the bidder has stringent budget restrictions"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Fixed-price contracts threaten quality (supplier cuts corners to stay in budget); T&amp;M contracts help quality because there is no price pressure.","topic":"t-contract"},{"id":"A19-10-8","src":"A19","wk":10,"n":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is a project proposal evaluation method?","opts":["Interviews","Demonstrations","Practical tests","All of the above"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Proposal evaluation methods: reading/scrutinising proposals, interviews, demonstrations, site visits, practical tests.","topic":"t-contract"},{"id":"A19-10-9","src":"A19","wk":10,"n":9,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is NOT true concerning the project closeout report?","opts":["It documents the important results obtained from various project closeout tasks","It starts with a historical summary of the project's deliverables and baseline activities over the course of the project","It presents the summary of the survey results and the quantitative data gathered about the project's performance","It does not contain any recommendation for improvement for other similar projects"],"ans":[3],"exp":"The closeout report DOES contain recommendations for improvement for similar projects (lessons learned, best practices).","topic":"t-closeout"},{"id":"A19-10-10","src":"A19","wk":10,"n":10,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following most accurately states the main aim of a post-project review?","opts":["Validate overall progress to date against the budget and schedule","Capture knowledge and document it for future usage","Ensure all documentation is accepted and signed by the sponsor","Establish that project benefits have been identified"],"ans":[1],"exp":"The post-implementation (post-mortem) review exists to learn: capture knowledge and document it for future projects.","topic":"t-closeout"},{"id":"A19-11-1","src":"A19","wk":11,"n":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is the basic focus of the modern quality assurance paradigms?","opts":["Process assurance","Product assurance","Thorough testing","Thorough testing and rejection of bad products"],"ans":[0],"exp":"The paradigm shifted from product assurance (inspect and reject) to process assurance: a good process followed rigorously gives good products.","topic":"t-quality"},{"id":"A19-11-2","src":"A19","wk":11,"n":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following ISO quality assurance standards applies to software development organizations?","opts":["ISO 9000","ISO 9001","ISO 9002","ISO 9003"],"ans":[1],"exp":"ISO 9001 applies to organisations that design, develop, produce and service: software developers. 9002 = production only; 9003 = inspection and testing only.","topic":"t-iso"},{"id":"A19-11-3","src":"A19","wk":11,"n":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A software organization has been assessed at SEI CMM Level 4. Which one of the following does the organization need to achieve for satisfying one of the KPAs of CMM Level 5?","opts":["Defect detection","Defect prevention","Defect isolation","Defect localization"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Level 5 KPAs: defect prevention, technology change management, process change management.","topic":"t-cmm"},{"id":"A19-11-4","src":"A19","wk":11,"n":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following can be considered to be the focus of 'quality control'?","opts":["All development work products are delivered on time and under budget","The development process is completely documented and adhered to during development","The performance of the development work products is measured","All development work products are thoroughly tested before delivery to the customer"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Quality control is product-oriented: test/inspect the work products and remove defects before delivery (also find causes).","topic":"t-quality"},{"id":"A19-11-5","src":"A19","wk":11,"n":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is expected to be observed in a software development organization assessed at the SEI CMM repeatable level?","opts":["Success in development of a software can be repeated","Success in development of a software can be repeated on similar software development projects","Success in development of a software can be repeated in all software development projects that the organization might undertake","When the same development team is chosen to develop another software, they can repeat their success"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Level 2 (Repeatable): basic project management (cost, schedule tracking); success can be repeated on projects SIMILAR to earlier ones.","topic":"t-cmm"},{"id":"A19-11-6","src":"A19","wk":11,"n":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is a requirement for ISO 9001 certification?","opts":["Demonstrate that the developed software has fulfilled its quality requirements","Submit the reliability assessment of all the developed software","Document the process being followed","Demonstrate that through stringent design and use of coding standards, testing is largely made redundant"],"ans":[2],"exp":"ISO 9001 requires a documented quality process that is actually followed; it does not certify products or require reliability figures.","topic":"t-iso"},{"id":"A19-11-7","src":"A19","wk":11,"n":7,"type":"mcq","text":"In CMMI, which one of the following is the name of maturity level 5?","opts":["Six Sigma","Optimized","Statistical","Optimizing"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Level 5 is called Optimizing (in both CMM and CMMI).","topic":"t-cmmi"},{"id":"A19-11-8","src":"A19","wk":11,"n":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following summarizes an important difference between quality assurance and quality control?","opts":["Thorough product testing","Ensuring that a good process is in place and has been documented","Ensuring that a good team is in place","Ensuring adequate usage of modern automated development tools"],"ans":[1],"exp":"QA ensures that a good process is in place, documented and followed (preventive). QC tests and inspects products (detective).","topic":"t-quality"},{"id":"A19-11-9","src":"A19","wk":11,"n":9,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following SEI CMM maturity levels should be recommended to a small startup company that focuses on outsourced software development and software maintenance work?","opts":["Level 1","Level 2","Level 4","Level 5"],"ans":[1],"exp":"A small outsourcing/maintenance company needs disciplined project management first: level 2 (repeatable). Higher levels are costly and not needed.","topic":"t-cmm"},{"id":"A19-11-10","src":"A19","wk":11,"n":10,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is a software defect?","opts":["A mistake made by a team member during requirements specification","A change that the team needs to make based on a finding during code review","A configuration management plan that is too elaborate","A change request that has been launched by the customer"],"ans":[1],"exp":"A defect (fault, bug) is something in a work product that must be changed; a finding from code review that requires a change is a defect. A mistake is the human error that causes it.","topic":"t-testing"},{"id":"A19-12-1","src":"A19","wk":12,"n":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is not a factor contributing to the increased difficulty of software reliability measurement, as compared to hardware reliability measurement?","opts":["The reliability improvement due to fixing a single bug depends on where the bug is located in the code","The perceived reliability of a software product is observer-dependent","The reliability of a software product keeps changing as errors are detected and fixed","Software is invisible in contrast to hardware, though the effect of execution of software can be observed"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Software reliability is hard to measure because improvement per fix depends on bug location (core/non-core), it is observer-dependent, and it changes with every fix. Invisibility is not the reason.","topic":"t-reliab"},{"id":"A19-12-2","src":"A19","wk":12,"n":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following can be said about the overall reliability behaviour of a typical software during its life time?","opts":["Initially increases, but later decreases","Initially decreases, but later increases","Increasing trend throughout the life cycle","Decreasing trend throughout the life cycle"],"ans":[0],"exp":"Typical life: reliability grows during testing/early use as bugs are fixed, then degrades as maintenance changes introduce new bugs (software failure curve).","topic":"t-reliab"},{"id":"A19-12-3","src":"A19","wk":12,"n":3,"type":"mcq","text":"After a software is maintained, which type of testing involves repeating tests that were already performed, to ensure that the code modifications made during maintenance have not introduced additional faults?","opts":["Beta testing","Functional testing","Bottom-up testing","Regression testing"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Regression testing reruns previous tests after a change to make sure nothing that worked is now broken.","topic":"t-testing"},{"id":"A19-12-4","src":"A19","wk":12,"n":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following statements is not an objective of software verification?","opts":["Ensuring that the software development steps are carried out correctly","Ensuring that the correct software has been developed","Achieving phase containment of errors","Ensuring that the outputs produced at a development stage conform to the outputs of the previous phase"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Verification: was each step done right (output of a phase conforms to its input), achieving phase containment. 'Did we build the correct software?' is validation.","topic":"t-testing"},{"id":"A19-12-5","src":"A19","wk":12,"n":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following is a practical use of reliability growth modelling?","opts":["Determine the operational life of an application software","Determine when to stop testing","Incorporate reliability growth information while designing","Incorporate reliability growth information in the code"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Reliability growth models predict when a target reliability will be reached, i.e. how long to test / when to stop testing.","topic":"t-relgrowth"},{"id":"A19-12-6","src":"A19","wk":12,"n":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is the objective of statistical testing?","opts":["Use statistical techniques to design test cases","Apply statistical techniques to the results of testing to determine the number of latent bugs in the software","Estimate software reliability","Apply statistical techniques to the results of testing to determine how long testing needs to be carried out"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Statistical testing uses the operational profile to estimate reliability, not to find as many bugs as possible.","topic":"t-relgrowth"},{"id":"A19-12-7","src":"A19","wk":12,"n":7,"type":"mcq","text":"What is the availability of a software with the following reliability figures? Mean Time Between Failures (MTBF) = 45 days, Mean Time To Repair (MTTR) = 5 hours.","opts":["10%","20%","90%","99%"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Availability = MTBF / (MTBF + MTTR) = (45 × 24 h) / (1080 + 5) = 1080/1085 ≈ 0.995 ≈ 99 %.","topic":"t-reliab"},{"id":"A19-12-8","src":"A19","wk":12,"n":8,"type":"mcq","text":"On 4000 service invocations, a software failed on 10 occasions. What is its POFOD (probability of failure on demand)?","opts":["4%","1%","0.25%","0.1%"],"ans":[2],"exp":"POFOD = failures / demands = 10 / 4000 = 0.0025 = 0.25 %.","topic":"t-reliab"},{"id":"A19-12-9","src":"A19","wk":12,"n":9,"type":"mcq","text":"On a bug fix, the reliability of a software may actually decrease rather than increase. Which one of the following explains this phenomenon?","opts":["The fixed bug is in the core part","The fixed bug is in the non-core part","The bug-fix induces several other bugs","The bug-fix is not proper"],"ans":[2],"exp":"If the bug fix itself introduces new bugs, reliability can drop: negative reliability growth (allowed by Littlewood-Verall).","topic":"t-reliab"},{"id":"A19-12-10","src":"A19","wk":12,"n":10,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following statements is FALSE?","opts":["Every failure is caused due to a fault","A fault is a synonym for a bug","An error may or may not cause a fault","Every mistake committed by a programmer shows up as a failure"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Not every mistake shows up as a failure: a fault may never be executed with failing inputs. Every failure is caused by some fault.","topic":"t-testing"},{"id":"AI-1-1","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":1,"n":1,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"According to the introductory lecture, which of the following best describes the 'software crisis'?","opts":["A shortage of available hardware to run new software.","The high cost of software licenses compared to hardware.","A persistent set of problems where projects are late, over budget, and fail to meet requirements.","The rapid obsolescence of programming languages."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Symptoms of the software crisis: fails to meet user requirements, expensive, difficult to alter/debug/enhance, often delivered late (CHAOS: only ~29 % succeed).","topic":"t-intro","lec":1},{"id":"AI-1-2","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":1,"n":2,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"Barry Boehm's W5HH principle is a framework used by project managers primarily to:","opts":["conduct performance reviews for team members.","comprehensively define and clarify the project scope.","select the appropriate software development lifecycle model.","negotiate contracts with external vendors."],"ans":[1],"exp":"W5HH (Boehm) = Why, What, When, Who, Where, How, How much: a question list that leads to the project scope.","topic":"t-intro","lec":9},{"id":"AI-1-3","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":1,"n":3,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is the key difference between a software product targeting a 'horizontal market' versus a 'vertical market'?","opts":["Horizontal market products are custom-built, while vertical market products are off-the-shelf.","Horizontal market products are for a wide range of industries, while vertical market products are for a specific industry.","Horizontal market products are information systems, while vertical market products are embedded systems.","Horizontal market products are more expensive than vertical market products."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Horizontal-market software meets the needs of many companies/industries; vertical-market software is designed for one particular industry.","topic":"t-intro","lec":9},{"id":"AI-1-4","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":1,"n":4,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"Which of the following is NOT one of the five core project management process groups as defined by PMBOK?","opts":["Initiating","Planning","Designing","Closing"],"ans":[2],"exp":"PMBOK process groups: Initiating, Planning, Executing, Monitoring &amp; Controlling, Closing. 'Designing' is a product activity, not a PM process group.","topic":"t-pm","lec":4},{"id":"AI-1-5","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":2,"n":5,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"In the classical waterfall model, which phase is stated to consume the maximum effort among all development and post-delivery phases?","opts":["Requirements Analysis","Design","Testing","Maintenance"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Among ALL phases maintenance takes the most effort; among the development phases, testing takes the most.","topic":"t-waterfall","lec":5},{"id":"AI-1-6","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":2,"n":6,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"What is the primary reason the V-Model is considered more suitable for high-reliability systems compared to the iterative waterfall model?","opts":["It allows for more frequent customer feedback.","It integrates test planning and design activities into every development phase, not just at the end.","It completely eliminates the possibility of defects.","It is a risk-driven model that uses prototyping."],"ans":[1],"exp":"V-model plans and designs tests in parallel with each development phase (requirements↔system test, HLD↔integration test, detailed design↔unit test): natural choice for safety-critical/high-reliability systems.","topic":"t-waterfall","lec":6},{"id":"AI-1-7","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":2,"n":7,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is the key characteristic that distinguishes the RAD model from the traditional prototyping model?","opts":["RAD uses a strict waterfall approach after prototyping.","In RAD, the prototype is refined and evolves into the final product, whereas in the prototyping model, it is typically thrown away.","RAD is only suitable for large, high-performance systems.","The prototyping model does not involve customer feedback."],"ans":[1],"exp":"In the prototyping model the prototype is usually thrown away; in RAD the quick prototype is refined increment by increment into the product.","topic":"t-proto","lec":7},{"id":"AI-1-8","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":2,"n":8,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"The Spiral Model is considered a 'meta-model' because:","opts":["it is the most recently developed model.","it is exclusively used for meta-data projects.","it can incorporate characteristics of waterfall, prototyping, and iterative models within its loops.","it guarantees zero risk in project development."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Spiral subsumes the other models: a single loop is a waterfall, iterations are evolutionary, prototyping is used for risk reduction. Hence 'meta-model'.","topic":"t-proto","lec":8},{"id":"AI-1-9","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":2,"n":9,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"In Scrum, what is the primary responsibility of the Product Owner?","opts":["To facilitate the daily scrum meeting and remove impediments.","To write the code for the features in the sprint backlog.","To represent the customer's interests, define features, and prioritize the Product Backlog.","To manage the technical architecture of the system."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Product owner: acts for the customer, defines features, decides release date/content, prioritises the backlog, accepts or rejects results.","topic":"t-agile","lec":10},{"id":"AI-1-10","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":2,"n":10,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"During a Daily Scrum meeting, which of the following questions is NOT one of the three key questions each team member answers?","opts":["What did I accomplish yesterday?","What is my plan for today?","How can we solve the technical problem I am facing?","Are there any obstacles in my way?"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Daily scrum: yesterday / today / obstacles. It is not a problem-solving session.","topic":"t-agile","lec":10},{"id":"AI-1-11","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":3,"n":11,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What is the primary purpose of a 'business case' or feasibility study?","opts":["To create a detailed technical design of the software.","To justify the project by demonstrating its financial and technical viability.","To hire the development team for the project.","To write the user manual for the final product."],"ans":[1],"exp":"The business case (feasibility study) justifies starting the project: benefits exceed development, implementation and operating costs, with business risks considered.","topic":"t-bizcase","lec":11},{"id":"AI-1-12","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":3,"n":12,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"Which project evaluation technique calculates the time required for a project's cumulative cash inflows to equal its initial investment but ignores all profitability after that point?","opts":["Net Present Value (NPV)","Return on Investment (ROI)","Payback Period","Net Profit"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Payback period = time for accumulated cash flow to become positive. It ignores everything after break-even (overall profitability).","topic":"t-cba","lec":13},{"id":"AI-1-13","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":3,"n":13,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"A project has an initial investment of $100,000, followed by five annual cash flows of $10,000, $10,000, $10,000, $20,000, and $100,000. According to the lecture's calculation using a 10% discount rate, what is the Net Present Value (NPV)?","opts":["$50,000","$618","$10,000","-$1,250"],"ans":[1],"exp":"NPV = −100,000 + 10,000×0.9091 + 10,000×0.8264 + 10,000×0.7513 + 20,000×0.6830 + 100,000×0.6209 = 618.","topic":"t-cba","lec":13},{"id":"AI-1-14","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":3,"n":14,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"In the context of program management, what is a 'program'?","opts":["A single, standalone software application.","A group of unrelated projects managed by the same person.","A group of related projects managed in a coordinated way to achieve benefits not possible if managed independently.","The source code written for a project."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Programme (Ferns): a group of projects managed in a coordinated way to gain benefits not possible if managed independently.","topic":"t-programme","lec":15},{"id":"AI-1-15","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":4,"n":15,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is the primary purpose of the Software Project Management Plan (SPMP) document?","opts":["It is the contract signed with the customer.","It is a marketing document for the final product.","It is the user manual for the software.","It formally records all project plans, estimates, schedules, and risk management strategies."],"ans":[3],"exp":"The SPMP documents the plans after planning: introduction, estimates, resource plan, schedules, risk management plan, tracking &amp; control plan, misc plans.","topic":"t-estbasics","lec":16},{"id":"AI-1-16","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":4,"n":16,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"According to the lecture, what is a major shortcoming of using Source Lines of Code (SLOC) as a size estimation metric?","opts":["It is too difficult to count.","It penalizes the use of high-level languages and concise coding styles.","It can only be used for object-oriented programming.","It is language-independent."],"ans":[1],"exp":"LOC penalises high-level languages, reuse and compact code; it is language- and programmer-dependent and hard to estimate early.","topic":"t-esttech","lec":17},{"id":"AI-1-17","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":4,"n":17,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"The Delphi estimation technique is designed to mitigate individual biases and groupthink by:","opts":["requiring all experts to agree on a single estimate in a face-to-face meeting.","using a mathematical formula based on historical data.","using multiple rounds of anonymous estimation and feedback facilitated by a coordinator.","averaging the estimates of the most senior and most junior team members."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Delphi: independent anonymous estimates, coordinator circulates the rationale, experts re-estimate over several rounds; experts never meet to argue.","topic":"t-esttech","lec":17},{"id":"AI-1-18","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":6,"n":18,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"In PERT/CPM, what does 'Slack' or 'Float' represent?","opts":["The total duration of the project.","The amount of time a non-critical activity can be delayed without affecting the project completion date.","The cost overrun on a specific task.","The number of resources assigned to a task."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Float/slack = how long an activity can be delayed without delaying the project end date: LS − ES = LF − EF.","topic":"t-cpm","lec":28},{"id":"AI-1-19","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":6,"n":19,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"During the backward pass in a PERT/CPM calculation, if an activity is a 'burst point' with multiple successors, how is its Latest Finish (LF) time determined?","opts":["It is the maximum of the Latest Start (LS) times of all its successors.","It is the sum of the Latest Start (LS) times of all its successors.","It is the minimum of the Latest Start (LS) times of all its successors.","It is equal to the project's overall earliest finish time."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Backward pass at a burst point: LF = the SMALLEST LS of the immediate successors.","topic":"t-cpm","lec":29},{"id":"AI-1-20","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":7,"n":20,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is the primary goal of 'Project Crashing'?","opts":["To intentionally fail a project to recover insurance costs.","To reduce the overall project duration by accelerating specific activities, usually at an increased cost.","To remove non-essential features from the project scope.","To re-assign all team members to different projects."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Crashing shortens critical activities by adding resources (people, overtime, faster tools), at extra cost, cheapest first.","topic":"t-pert","lec":31},{"id":"AI-1-21","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":7,"n":21,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"According to Herzberg's Two-Factor Theory, which of the following is a 'Motivator' (or satisfier)?","opts":["Company policy","Salary","Working conditions","Recognition of effort and performance"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Motivators (job content): recognition, the job itself, achievement, promotion. Pay, policy and working conditions are hygiene factors.","topic":"t-people","lec":32},{"id":"AI-1-22","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":7,"n":22,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"What is the primary disadvantage of a 'Chief Programmer Team' structure?","opts":["Decision-making is too slow due to extensive discussions.","It is only suitable for very large and complex projects.","The project is highly dependent on a single individual, creating a single point of failure.","It leads to poor code quality due to a lack of expertise."],"ans":[2],"exp":"The chief programmer makes all technical decisions and writes the critical code: if he/she leaves or errs, the project suffers (single point of failure).","topic":"t-org","lec":34},{"id":"AI-1-23","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":8,"n":23,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What is the primary purpose of a resource histogram in project management?","opts":["To track the project budget over time.","To visualize the demand for a specific resource over the project's duration.","To list the dependencies between project activities.","To document the project's final deliverables."],"ans":[1],"exp":"A resource histogram shows the level of demand for one resource type over time; it is used to spot peaks and smooth them.","topic":"t-resource","lec":37},{"id":"AI-1-24","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":9,"n":24,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"In Earned Value Analysis (EVA), what does a Cost Performance Index (CPI) of less than 1 indicate?","opts":["The project is under budget.","The project is ahead of schedule.","The project is over budget.","The project is exactly on schedule and on budget."],"ans":[2],"exp":"CPI = EV/AC &lt; 1: less value earned than money spent → over budget.","topic":"t-eva","lec":44},{"id":"AI-1-25","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":10,"n":25,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What is the primary function of a Change Control Board (CCB)?","opts":["To write the initial project requirements.","To test the final software product.","To review and approve proposed changes to controlled work products.","To manage the project's budget."],"ans":[2],"exp":"The CCB reviews a changed configuration item and authorises the restore (motivated, documented, tested, consistent with others' changes).","topic":"t-scm","lec":47},{"id":"AI-1-26","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":10,"n":26,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is the key difference between a 'Fixed Price' contract and a 'Time and Material' contract?","opts":["Fixed price contracts are only for hardware, while time and material are for software.","In a fixed price contract, the customer has budget certainty, while in a time and material contract, the customer bears the financial risk of overruns.","Fixed price contracts are more flexible for changing requirements.","Time and material contracts motivate suppliers to be more cost-effective."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Fixed price: customer knows the expenditure, supplier carries the risk. Time &amp; materials: customer pays per unit of effort and absorbs the risk of changing requirements.","topic":"t-contract","lec":48},{"id":"AI-1-27","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":10,"n":27,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What is the primary purpose of a post-implementation project review, also known as a 'post-mortem analysis'?","opts":["To assign blame for project failures.","To calculate the final financial profit or loss of the project.","To identify successes, failures, and lessons learned to improve future projects.","To release the final software version to the public."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Post-implementation review (post-mortem): critical analysis to learn lessons and improve future projects, not to blame.","topic":"t-closeout","lec":50},{"id":"AI-1-28","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":11,"n":28,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"What is a fundamental distinction between ISO 9001 certification and SEI CMM assessment?","opts":["ISO 9001 is for software only, while SEI CMM is for all industries.","ISO 9001 provides a step-by-step improvement path, while SEI CMM is a go/no-go certification.","ISO 9001 is a generic quality assurance standard, while SEI CMM is a software-specific framework providing a gradual path for process maturity improvement.","Only SEI CMM requires configuration management."],"ans":[2],"exp":"ISO 9001: generic standard, certification, single level. SEI CMM: software-specific, five levels with KPAs giving a gradual improvement path; ISO 9001 ≈ CMM level 3.","topic":"t-cmm","lec":55},{"id":"AI-1-29","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":12,"n":29,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"In the context of software reliability, what does the '90-10 rule' state?","opts":["90% of bugs are found by 10% of testers.","A project is 90% complete with 10% of the work remaining.","Approximately 90% of a program's execution time is spent in only 10% of its code.","90% of development effort is spent on coding, and 10% on testing."],"ans":[2],"exp":"90-10 rule: 90 % of execution time is spent in 10 % of the instructions (the core). Fixing bugs in the non-core changes perceived reliability little.","topic":"t-reliab","lec":56},{"id":"AI-1-30","src":"AI","paper":1,"wk":5,"n":30,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"According to Putnam's model, if a project's development time (td) is compressed by half, the total effort (K) required increases by a factor of:","opts":["2","4","8","16"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Putnam: K = C₁/td⁴. Halving td multiplies effort by 2⁴ = 16.","topic":"t-staffing","lec":25},{"id":"AI-2-1","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":1,"n":1,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"According to the lecture, what is the 'software crisis'?","opts":["A shortage of hardware to run modern software applications.","A persistent set of problems including projects failing to meet requirements, exceeding budgets, and being delivered late.","The rapid increase in software costs relative to hardware costs.","The difficulty in finding developers trained in older programming languages."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Software crisis: late, over budget, failing requirements, hard to maintain.","topic":"t-intro","lec":1},{"id":"AI-2-2","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":1,"n":2,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What is a project stakeholder, as defined in the lecture?","opts":["Only the customers who pay for the software project.","Only the project manager and the development team.","Individuals or organizations actively involved in a project, or whose interests may be affected by its execution or completion.","The top management of the company that initiates the project."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Stakeholders: individuals and organisations actively involved in the project or whose interests may be affected by its execution or completion.","topic":"t-intro","lec":2},{"id":"AI-2-3","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":1,"n":3,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"The '99 percent complete syndrome' refers to a situation where:","opts":["A project is successfully completed with 99% of its features.","A project is perpetually reported as 'almost done' by team members, leading to significant delays.","99% of the project budget has been spent before completion.","The final product has a 99% defect-free rate."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Without a life cycle model (milestones) managers rely on developers' guesses, and work stays '99 % complete' for a long time.","topic":"t-pm","lec":5},{"id":"AI-2-4","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":2,"n":4,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What is the principle of 'phase containment of errors'?","opts":["Containing all project errors within a single, dedicated testing phase.","The idea that errors should be detected and corrected as close as possible to the phase in which they were introduced.","A method for ensuring that errors in one module do not affect other modules.","The principle that a certain number of errors is acceptable in each phase."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Phase containment: detect and correct errors in the same phase in which they are introduced; the later a defect is found, the costlier it is.","topic":"t-waterfall","lec":6},{"id":"AI-2-5","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":2,"n":5,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"According to the lecture, what is a major criticism of the Waterfall Model?","opts":["It requires too much customer involvement throughout the project.","It is too flexible, leading to scope creep and chaos.","It is a 'heavy-weight process' with extensive documentation overhead and inflexibility to change requests.","It is only suitable for very small, trivial projects."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Waterfall-based models are 'heavy-weight': requirements frozen up front, documentation-heavy, poor at change requests (40 % of requirements change).","topic":"t-proto","lec":7},{"id":"AI-2-6","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":2,"n":6,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What is the core philosophy of the evolutionary model of software development?","opts":["To define and freeze all requirements before development begins.","To develop core modules initially and then progressively refine and expand them through multiple iterations based on user feedback.","To complete the entire system in a single, large release after extensive testing.","To focus exclusively on risk mitigation through prototyping."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Evolutionary: build the core modules first, then refine and add capability in successive versions using user feedback.","topic":"t-proto","lec":21},{"id":"AI-2-7","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":2,"n":7,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"In the context of Extreme Programming (XP), what is 'pair programming'?","opts":["Two programmers dividing the work and coding separate modules in parallel.","A lead programmer mentoring a junior programmer.","Two programmers working together at one workstation, with one writing code and the other reviewing it.","A formal code review process conducted by a pair of senior developers."],"ans":[2],"exp":"XP takes code review to the extreme: pair programming, two programmers at one workstation, one codes while the other reviews.","topic":"t-agile","lec":9},{"id":"AI-2-8","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":2,"n":8,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"In the Scrum framework, who is the Product Owner?","opts":["The most senior developer on the team who owns the codebase.","The project manager responsible for the team's daily tasks.","A team member responsible for facilitating the Scrum process and removing impediments.","A team member who acts as the voice of the customer, defines features, and prioritizes the Product Backlog."],"ans":[3],"exp":"The Product Owner represents the customer's interests and owns/prioritises the product backlog.","topic":"t-agile","lec":10},{"id":"AI-2-9","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":4,"n":9,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What is the 'Price to Win' estimation method?","opts":["A technique where the project cost is estimated based on the most competitive market price.","A method where the project cost is set at whatever the customer is willing or able to spend.","An estimation based on winning a government contract through the lowest possible bid.","A detailed bottom-up estimation to ensure the price is high enough to guarantee a win."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Price to win: the project costs whatever the customer can spend; you get the contract but the cost may not reflect the work.","topic":"t-esttech","lec":17},{"id":"AI-2-10","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":5,"n":10,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What are the three development modes in the Basic COCOMO 81 model?","opts":["Simple, Moderate, Complex","Small, Medium, Large","Organic, Semi-detached, Embedded","Basic, Intermediate, Detailed"],"ans":[2],"exp":"COCOMO 81 modes: organic, semi-detached, embedded (models: basic, intermediate, detailed).","topic":"t-cocomo","lec":8},{"id":"AI-2-11","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":2,"n":11,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is the primary purpose of a feasibility study in the project management lifecycle?","opts":["To create a detailed project schedule and allocate resources.","To determine if a project is technically viable and financially worthwhile before significant resources are committed.","To gather all customer requirements for the software product.","To select the development team and assign roles and responsibilities."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Feasibility study: is the software technically feasible and financially worthwhile? It ends with a go/no-go decision.","topic":"t-waterfall","lec":3},{"id":"AI-2-12","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":1,"n":12,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is the key difference between project management processes and product-oriented processes?","opts":["Project management processes are done by the customer, while product-oriented processes are done by the development team.","Project management processes focus on planning and control, while product-oriented processes focus on creating the actual project deliverables.","Project management processes are only performed at the beginning of a project, while product-oriented processes are continuous.","There is no significant difference; the terms are used interchangeably."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Project management processes plan and control the work; product-oriented processes specify and create the product.","topic":"t-pm","lec":4},{"id":"AI-2-13","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":3,"n":13,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is the key difference between Project Risk and Business Risk as defined in the lecture?","opts":["Project Risk affects the budget, while Business Risk affects the schedule.","Project Risk relates to threats to the successful execution of the project itself, while Business Risk threatens the benefits of the delivered project.","Project Risk is managed by the project manager, while Business Risk is managed by the customer.","Project Risk is technical in nature, while Business Risk is purely financial."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Project risk: threats to successful execution. Business risk: threats to the benefits of the delivered project (the business case focuses on business risk).","topic":"t-bizcase","lec":11},{"id":"AI-2-14","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":3,"n":14,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is a key difference between the Payback Period and Net Present Value (NPV) as project evaluation techniques?","opts":["Payback Period considers overall profitability, while NPV only considers the time to recover investment.","Payback Period is more complex to calculate than NPV.","NPV accounts for the time value of money by discounting future cash flows, while Payback Period does not.","NPV is expressed in years, while Payback Period is a percentage."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Payback ignores the time value of money; NPV discounts every cash flow with DF = 1/(1+r)^t.","topic":"t-cba","lec":13},{"id":"AI-2-15","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":3,"n":15,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"In the context of risk evaluation, how is a 'risk premium' used when calculating the Net Present Value (NPV) for a risky project?","opts":["It is a bonus paid to the project team for managing risks successfully.","It is an additional amount added to the project's expected benefits to account for potential gains.","It is an additional percentage added to the discount rate, making riskier projects appear less attractive unless their returns are higher.","It is a separate fund set aside to cover any financial losses from risks."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Risk premium: add, e.g., 2 % (fairly safe) to 5 % (fairly risky) to the discount rate, so a risky project needs higher returns to show a positive NPV.","topic":"t-riskeval","lec":14},{"id":"AI-2-16","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":3,"n":16,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"How does a Program Manager's role differ from a Project Manager's role regarding resource optimization?","opts":["A Program Manager minimizes resource demand for their specific project, while a Project Manager optimizes across all projects.","A Program Manager aims for the optimal use of resources across all projects within the program, while a Project Manager seeks to minimize resource demand for their single project.","A Program Manager focuses only on financial resources, while a Project Manager focuses on human resources.","There is no difference; both roles have the same objective for resource optimization."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Programme manager: optimise resource use across many projects (personal relationships with skilled staff). Project manager: minimise demand for his one project.","topic":"t-programme","lec":15},{"id":"AI-2-17","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":4,"n":17,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is a primary disadvantage of the bottom-up estimation technique?","opts":["It is very fast to implement but often inaccurate.","It fails to consider the complexities of individual low-level tasks.","It can be very time-consuming and may overlook system-level costs like integration and configuration management.","It requires extensive historical data from similar past projects to be effective."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Bottom-up is time-consuming, needs detail early and tends to miss system-level costs (integration, CM, QA).","topic":"t-esttech","lec":18},{"id":"AI-2-18","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":4,"n":18,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is the purpose of the Value Adjustment Factor (VAF) in the Albrecht/IFPUG function point calculation?","opts":["To convert the final function point count into Lines of Code (LOC).","To determine the initial raw count of function points based on user inputs and outputs.","To refine the Unadjusted Function Point (UFP) count by accounting for 14 general system characteristics related to technical and environmental complexity.","To calculate the total cost of the project based on the function point size."],"ans":[2],"exp":"VAF = 0.65 + 0.01 × TDI, from 14 general system characteristics rated 0-5. AFP = UFP × VAF.","topic":"t-fp","lec":19},{"id":"AI-2-19","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":4,"n":19,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"COSMIC Full Function Points (FFPs) are designed for embedded and real-time systems. What are the four fundamental types of data movement that this method counts?","opts":["Inputs, Outputs, Inquiries, and Files","Creates, Reads, Updates, and Deletes","Entries, Exits, Reads, and Writes","Screens, Reports, Modules, and Interfaces"],"ans":[2],"exp":"COSMIC data movements: entries, exits, reads, writes; each counts 1 Cfsu.","topic":"t-fp","lec":20},{"id":"AI-2-20","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":5,"n":20,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is the purpose of calculating Program Volume (V) in Halstead's Software Science?","opts":["To measure the physical disk space occupied by the program.","To count the total number of operators and operands used in the program.","To compensate for the effect of the programming language used, providing a more standardized measure of program size in bits.","To estimate the number of mental discriminations required to write the program."],"ans":[2],"exp":"V = N log₂ η: minimum number of bits needed to encode the program; compensates for the language used.","topic":"t-halstead","lec":24},{"id":"AI-2-21","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":5,"n":21,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"How does Putnam's model demonstrate the concept of 'schedule compression'?","opts":["It shows that reducing development time by half will also cut the required effort in half.","It indicates that compressing the schedule has a linear and predictable impact on project cost.","It shows a highly non-linear relationship where reducing development time leads to a disproportionately large increase in required effort (effort scales with td^4).","It proves that schedule compression is impossible beyond a 10% reduction from the nominal time."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Putnam: effort ∝ 1/td⁴, so a small schedule compression costs a large increase in effort.","topic":"t-staffing","lec":25},{"id":"AI-2-22","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":6,"n":22,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"Why is it important for a project manager to avoid both too fine and too coarse granularity when creating a Work Breakdown Structure (WBS)?","opts":["Too fine granularity is expensive, while too coarse granularity is too simple.","Too fine granularity creates excessive management overhead, while too coarse granularity leads to a loss of project control and visibility.","Too fine granularity is only for agile projects, while too coarse granularity is for waterfall projects.","Too fine granularity requires expert developers, while too coarse granularity can be handled by junior developers."],"ans":[1],"exp":"WBS granularity: too fine = management overhead; too coarse = poor control and visibility. Lowest level ≈ one or two weeks of one person's work.","topic":"t-wbs","lec":26},{"id":"AI-2-23","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":8,"n":23,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"How does the concept of 'slack' or 'float time' in PERT/CPM provide flexibility for a project manager in resource management?","opts":["It allows the project manager to add extra features to activities with slack time.","It indicates which activities can be eliminated from the project to save time.","It allows the project manager to strategically reallocate resources from non-critical activities (with slack) to critical activities that are falling behind.","It represents the extra budget available for non-critical activities."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Non-critical activities have float, so their resources can be shifted to critical activities that are slipping.","topic":"t-resource","lec":27},{"id":"AI-2-24","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":7,"n":24,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"During project crashing, why must the critical path be re-evaluated after each incremental reduction in a task's duration?","opts":["To ensure the project budget is not exceeded.","Because reducing the duration of a critical task can cause a different, previously non-critical path to become the new critical path.","To confirm that the customer still requires the project to be accelerated.","To allow the project manager to add more tasks to the project."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Crashing one path can make a parallel path critical, so recompute the critical path after every step.","topic":"t-pert","lec":31},{"id":"AI-2-25","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":7,"n":25,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"How does the Oldham-Hackman Job Characteristic Model suggest a manager can redesign a job to make it more meaningful and motivating?","opts":["By increasing the salary and improving working conditions.","By implementing stricter supervision and control over tasks.","By increasing skill variety, task identity, task significance, autonomy, and feedback.","By reducing the number of tasks to minimize employee workload."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Oldham-Hackman: skill variety, task identity, task significance (meaningfulness) + autonomy + feedback.","topic":"t-people","lec":32},{"id":"AI-2-26","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":7,"n":26,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"According to Boehm's list of frequent risks, what is 'gold plating' and how can it be mitigated?","opts":["It is the risk of using expensive hardware; it can be mitigated by choosing cheaper alternatives.","It is the risk of developers adding features not specified in requirements; it can be mitigated by requirement scrubbing and prototyping for user evaluation.","It is the risk of delivering the project late; it can be mitigated by adding more developers to the team.","It is the risk of the user interface being poorly designed; it can be mitigated by hiring a dedicated UI/UX expert."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Gold plating = developers adding unrequested features. Boehm's remedy: requirements scrubbing, prototyping, cost-benefit analysis.","topic":"t-risk","lec":34},{"id":"AI-2-27","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":8,"n":27,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"The lecture advises not to add new dependencies to the activity network to reflect resource constraints. Why is this the recommended practice?","opts":["Because resource constraints are not as important as task dependencies.","Because adding such dependencies makes the network messy, and if the resource constraint disappears, the artificial link remains, causing confusion.","Because modern software tools cannot handle resource-based dependencies.","Because it violates the fundamental rules of PERT/CPM network construction."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Do not add resource-induced links to the activity network: it becomes messy and the link stays after the constraint disappears. Change dates on the schedule instead.","topic":"t-resource","lec":37},{"id":"AI-2-28","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":9,"n":28,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"The lecture mentions that Earned Value Analysis (EVA) has not achieved universal acceptance in software development. What is the primary reason for this challenge?","opts":["EVA calculations are too complex for most project managers to perform.","EVA is only suitable for hardware projects and cannot be applied to intangible products.","A 'half-completed software project' often has no functional value until it is fully deployed, making it difficult to assign meaningful 'earned value' to partial work.","The US Department of Defense has prohibited the use of EVA for software projects."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Objection: a half-built house has value, a half-built program has little. (This misunderstands EVA, which tracks budgeted cost of completed work.)","topic":"t-eva","lec":44},{"id":"AI-2-29","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":9,"n":29,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"What are the potential consequences and risks of altering activity precedence requirements to get a project back on target?","opts":["It always reduces project costs and improves team morale.","It has no impact on the project other than accelerating the schedule.","It can compromise quality and increase project risk, such as starting coding before design is complete, which can lead to extensive rework.","It is a forbidden practice in all project management methodologies."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Relaxing precedence (e.g. coding before design is final) can save time but compromises quality and raises risk of rework.","topic":"t-backontrack","lec":45},{"id":"AI-2-30","src":"AI","paper":2,"wk":11,"n":30,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"The lecture states that an organization cannot advertise its products as 'ISO 9000 certified.' Why is this the case, and what is the underlying assumption of ISO 9000 certification?","opts":["Because ISO 9000 is an outdated standard that has been replaced by SEI CMM.","Because advertising individual products is too expensive for most ISO certified companies.","Because ISO certification is awarded to an organization's PROCESS, not its products, based on the assumption that a good process leads to a good product.","Because only the ISO organization itself is allowed to advertise certified products, not the companies that make them."],"ans":[2],"exp":"ISO 9000 certifies an organisation's process, not products. Advertising products as ISO-certified risks withdrawal of the certificate.","topic":"t-iso","lec":52},{"id":"AI-3-1","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":1,"n":1,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"According to the lecture, the \"software crisis\" refers to a persistent set of problems in software development. Which of the following is NOT a symptom of this crisis?","opts":["Projects exceeding their budget.","Software that is difficult to alter or maintain.","Hardware costs increasing faster than software costs.","Projects being delivered late."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Hardware cost has fallen relative to software cost; rising hardware cost is not a symptom of the software crisis.","topic":"t-intro","lec":1},{"id":"AI-3-2","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":2,"n":2,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What is the primary purpose of conducting a feasibility study at the beginning of a project?","opts":["To write the initial lines of code for the project.","To determine if the project is viable and should be undertaken.","To hire the entire development team.","To finalize the user interface design."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Feasibility study: determine whether the project is technically feasible and financially worthwhile before committing resources.","topic":"t-waterfall","lec":5},{"id":"AI-3-3","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":1,"n":3,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"The \"Build and Fix Model\" is described as being suitable for which type of projects?","opts":["Large, complex projects with many team members.","All projects that require high reliability.","Trivial projects, such as student assignments.","Projects with a fixed, unchangeable budget."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Build-and-fix (exploratory) works only when one programmer can grasp the whole problem: trivial programs such as student assignments.","topic":"t-pm","lec":4},{"id":"AI-3-4","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":2,"n":4,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"In the Scrum framework, what is a \"Sprint\"?","opts":["A daily 15-minute meeting for the development team.","The final phase of the project where all testing is done.","A prioritized list of all desired features for a product.","A fixed-duration iteration during which a product increment is developed."],"ans":[3],"exp":"Sprint: fixed-length (≈ one month) time-box producing a potentially shippable increment; no changes entertained during the sprint.","topic":"t-agile","lec":10},{"id":"AI-3-5","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":6,"n":5,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What does the acronym PERT stand for in the context of project scheduling?","opts":["Project Execution and Resource Tracking.","Program Evaluation and Review Technique.","Product Engineering and Revision Tool.","Process Enhancement and Reporting Technology."],"ans":[1],"exp":"PERT = Program Evaluation and Review Technique (US Navy, Polaris missile). CPM = Critical Path Method (DuPont).","topic":"t-wbs","lec":27},{"id":"AI-3-6","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":4,"n":6,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What is the primary goal of the \"Price to Win\" estimation strategy?","opts":["To accurately calculate the total effort required for the project.","To set the project cost based on what the customer is willing to spend.","To estimate the project size using Function Points.","To ensure the highest possible quality for the final product."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Price to win: cost set to what the customer is willing to spend.","topic":"t-esttech","lec":17},{"id":"AI-3-7","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":9,"n":7,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"According to the lecture, what is a primary use of a Gantt chart in project management?","opts":["To write and compile source code.","To conduct user acceptance testing.","To plan the utilization of various resources.","To formally close a contract with a vendor."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Gantt charts help plan resource utilisation; PERT charts help monitor timely progress.","topic":"t-visual","lec":42},{"id":"AI-3-8","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":7,"n":8,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What is the core concept of \"egoless programming\" within a democratic team structure?","opts":["Only the most senior programmer is allowed to write code.","No single individual \"owns\" a piece of code; work is reviewed by all.","Programmers work in complete isolation to avoid conflicts.","All technical decisions are made by the project manager."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Egoless programming (democratic team): code ownership is shared and code is exchanged for review.","topic":"t-org","lec":33},{"id":"AI-3-9","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":12,"n":9,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What is the fundamental difference between a \"fault\" and a \"failure\" in software testing?","opts":["A fault is a human mistake, while a failure is a bug in the code.","A fault is an incorrect step in the code, while a failure is the observable deviation from expected behavior.","A fault can only be found by the developer, while a failure is found by the user.","A fault and a failure are synonyms for the same problem."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Error/mistake (human) → fault/defect/bug (in the code) → failure (observable wrong behaviour). A fault may never cause a failure.","topic":"t-testing","lec":60},{"id":"AI-3-10","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":11,"n":10,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What does ISO 9001 certification primarily signify about an organization?","opts":["That its individual software products are guaranteed to be bug-free.","That the organization has a documented quality process or system.","That the organization is the most profitable in its industry.","That all its employees have passed a specific quality exam."],"ans":[1],"exp":"ISO 9001 certification says a documented quality system exists and is followed; it says nothing directly about product quality.","topic":"t-iso","lec":52},{"id":"AI-3-11","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":1,"n":11,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"According to Barry Boehm's W5HH principle for defining project scope, which question specifically addresses the project's budget and resource requirements?","opts":["Why is the software being built?","What will be done?","Who is responsible for a function?","How much of each resource is needed?"],"ans":[3],"exp":"W5HH 'How much of each resource is needed?' covers budget and resources.","topic":"t-intro","lec":9},{"id":"AI-3-12","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":2,"n":12,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"In the V-Model of software development, the design of integration test cases is typically carried out in parallel with which development phase?","opts":["Requirements Specification","High-Level Design","Detailed Design","Coding"],"ans":[1],"exp":"V-model pairs: requirements ↔ system test design, high-level design ↔ integration test design, detailed design ↔ unit test design.","topic":"t-waterfall","lec":6},{"id":"AI-3-13","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":2,"n":13,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is the key difference between a purely incremental model and an evolutionary model?","opts":["The incremental model uses iterations, while the evolutionary model does not.","The evolutionary model delivers working software to the customer, while the incremental model does not.","In the incremental model all requirements are defined upfront, while in the evolutionary model they emerge over time.","The incremental model is for large projects, while the evolutionary model is only for small projects."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Incremental: requirements known up front, split into increments. Evolutionary: requirements, plans and solution evolve over iterations.","topic":"t-proto","lec":21},{"id":"AI-3-14","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":3,"n":14,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"Which project evaluation technique calculates the difference between total income and total costs but is criticized for not accounting for the timing of cash flows?","opts":["Net Present Value (NPV)","Internal Rate of Return (IRR)","Payback Period","Net Profit"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Net profit = total income − total cost over the life of the project; it ignores timing of cash flows.","topic":"t-cba","lec":13},{"id":"AI-3-15","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":4,"n":15,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is a major shortcoming of using Source Lines of Code (SLOC) as a project size estimation metric?","opts":["It is too difficult for non-technical stakeholders to understand.","It can only be used for projects written in assembly language.","It penalizes the use of high-level languages and code reuse.","It requires expensive, specialized tools to calculate."],"ans":[2],"exp":"LOC penalises high-level languages and reuse.","topic":"t-esttech","lec":17},{"id":"AI-3-16","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":5,"n":16,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"In the COCOMO 81 model, how does the \"Embedded Mode\" of development typically differ from the \"Organic Mode\"?","opts":["Embedded mode projects are smaller and use experienced teams, while organic mode projects are large and innovative.","Embedded mode is for projects with flexible constraints, while organic mode has tight constraints.","Embedded mode projects are completely new and innovative, while organic mode projects are familiar and stable.","The constants used for effort estimation are lower for embedded mode than for organic mode."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Embedded: new, innovative, tight constraints (OS, real-time). Organic: familiar, stable, similar to past products (accounting).","topic":"t-cocomo","lec":8},{"id":"AI-3-17","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":8,"n":17,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is the primary goal of \"resource smoothing\" in project management?","opts":["To increase the total number of resources available to the project.","To ensure all activities start on their earliest possible date.","To create a more even and consistent demand for resources over time.","To identify the project's critical path."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Resource smoothing delays/splits non-critical activities so that demand is even and within availability.","topic":"t-resource","lec":37},{"id":"AI-3-18","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":6,"n":18,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"In PERT/CPM, when calculating the Earliest Start (ES) time for an activity that has multiple predecessors (a merge point), what rule is applied?","opts":["The ES is the average of the Earliest Finish (EF) times of all its predecessors.","The ES is the earliest (minimum) of the EF times of all its predecessors.","The ES is the latest (maximum) of the EF times of all its predecessors.","The ES is always set to zero."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Forward pass at a merge point: ES = the LATEST (maximum) EF of the predecessors.","topic":"t-cpm","lec":29},{"id":"AI-3-19","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":7,"n":19,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"According to Herzberg's Two-Factor Theory, what is the primary role of \"hygiene factors\" such as pay and company policy in employee motivation?","opts":["They are the main drivers of job satisfaction and high performance.","They have no impact on employee satisfaction or dissatisfaction.","Their absence causes dissatisfaction, but their presence does not actively motivate.","They are only important for employees at the self-actualization level."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Hygiene factors (pay, policy, conditions) prevent dissatisfaction but do not motivate.","topic":"t-people","lec":32},{"id":"AI-3-20","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":9,"n":20,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"A project has an Earned Value (EV) of $50,000, a Planned Value (PV) of $60,000, and an Actual Cost (AC) of $55,000. Based on these figures, what is the status of the project?","opts":["Ahead of schedule and under budget.","Behind schedule and over budget.","Ahead of schedule and over budget.","Behind schedule and under budget."],"ans":[1],"exp":"SV = 50k − 60k &lt; 0 (behind schedule); CV = 50k − 55k &lt; 0 (over budget).","topic":"t-eva","lec":44},{"id":"AI-3-21","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":10,"n":21,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is the primary advantage of using a restricted tendering process compared to an open tendering process for contractor selection?","opts":["It guarantees the lowest possible price due to maximum competition.","It is mandated by law for all government projects.","It reduces the evaluation time and effort by soliciting bids only from pre-selected suppliers.","It eliminates the need for a formal contract."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Restricted tendering: only invited suppliers bid; the customer can cut the list at any stage, so evaluation is cheaper and faster than open tendering.","topic":"t-contract","lec":48},{"id":"AI-3-22","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":11,"n":22,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"In the SEI CMM framework, what is the key characteristic that distinguishes a Level 3 (Defined) organization from a Level 2 (Repeatable) organization?","opts":["Level 3 uses quantitative quality goals, while Level 2 does not.","Level 3 has processes that are documented and standardized across the organization, while Level 2's processes are project-specific.","Level 3 relies on individual heroics, while Level 2 has basic project management.","Level 3 focuses on continuous process improvement, while Level 2 focuses on repeating success."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Level 2: basic project management, process may vary between projects. Level 3: defined, documented, organisation-wide standard process.","topic":"t-cmm","lec":54},{"id":"AI-3-23","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":2,"n":23,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"A project manager uses the Value-to-Cost (V/C) ratio to prioritize increments. Given the following functionalities and their customer-rated value (V) and developer-rated cost (C), which should be developed first? Profit Reports (V=9, C=2), Online Database (V=1, C=10), Ad Hoc Enquiry (V=5, C=5), Profit-Based Pay (V=9, C=1).","opts":["Profit Reports","Online Database","Ad Hoc Enquiry","Profit-Based Pay"],"ans":[3],"exp":"V/C: profit-based pay 9/1 = 9 (1st), profit reports 9/2 = 4.5, purchasing plans 2.25, ad hoc enquiry 1, online database 0.11.","topic":"t-proto","lec":7},{"id":"AI-3-24","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":4,"n":24,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"In the context of the Delphi estimation technique, what is the primary purpose of ensuring anonymity among experts during the estimation rounds?","opts":["To save time by avoiding lengthy group discussions.","To prevent a dominant personality from unduly influencing the group's estimates.","To make it easier for the coordinator to calculate the final average.","To allow experts to use different estimation formulas without conflict."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Anonymity in Delphi stops dominant personalities and group-think from biasing estimates.","topic":"t-esttech","lec":17},{"id":"AI-3-25","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":4,"n":25,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"A project is estimated using the Albrecht/IFPUG method. It has 50 user inputs, 40 user outputs, 35 inquiries, 6 internal files, and 4 external interfaces, all of average complexity. If the Total Degree of Influence (TDI) is calculated to be 42, what is the final Adjusted Function Point (AFP) count? (Use average weights: EI=4, EO=5, EQ=4, LIF=10, EIF=7).","opts":["628","672","618","661"],"ans":[1],"exp":"UFP = 50×4 + 40×5 + 35×4 + 6×10 + 4×7 = 628; VAF = 0.65 + 0.42 = 1.07; AFP = 628 × 1.07 ≈ 672.","topic":"t-fp","lec":20},{"id":"AI-3-26","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":5,"n":26,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"According to Putnam's estimation model, the relationship between effort (K) and development time (td) is K = C1 / td^4. If a project's schedule is compressed to half of its original planned time, by what factor will the total effort and cost increase?","opts":["2 times","4 times","8 times","16 times"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Putnam: (1/0.5)⁴ = 16.","topic":"t-staffing","lec":25},{"id":"AI-3-27","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":7,"n":27,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"A project manager needs to crash a project. The current critical path is A-B-D with a duration of 25 weeks. A parallel path, A-C-E, has a duration of 24 weeks. The costs to crash each task per week are: A=$100, B=$150, C=$80, D=$120. Which task should the manager crash first to reduce the project duration by one week?","opts":["Task A, because it is the cheapest on the critical path.","Task C, because it is the cheapest overall.","Task D, because it is on the critical path.","Tasks A and C must be crashed simultaneously."],"ans":[0],"exp":"A lies on both A-B-D (25) and A-C-E (24): crashing A by one week shortens both paths and is the cheapest critical activity ($100).","topic":"t-pert","lec":31},{"id":"AI-3-28","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":12,"n":28,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"What is the primary reason that the hardware \"bathtub curve\" of reliability, which includes burn-in, useful life, and wear-out phases, is not directly applicable to software?","opts":["Software is more complex than hardware.","Software failures are caused by design flaws (bugs), not physical wear and tear.","Software reliability cannot be measured, whereas hardware reliability can.","Hardware warranties cover the burn-in period, but software has no warranties."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Software does not wear out; failures come from design faults. Its failure rate falls as bugs are fixed and can rise with changes.","topic":"t-reliab","lec":58},{"id":"AI-3-29","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":9,"n":29,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"During a formal code inspection, which of the following error types is a primary target for discovery, as opposed to an error that is typically caught by a compiler?","opts":["A missing semicolon at the end of a statement.","Use of an uninitialized variable in a calculation.","An incorrectly spelled keyword like \"whle\" instead of \"while\".","A function call with the wrong number of arguments."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Inspection looks for common programmer mistakes (uninitialised variables, wrong operators, array bounds, parameter mismatch). The compiler catches syntax errors.","topic":"t-codereview","lec":41},{"id":"AI-3-30","src":"AI","paper":3,"wk":11,"n":30,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"What is the core assumption of Quality Assurance (QA) that represents a fundamental shift from earlier Quality Control (QC) methods?","opts":["That testing every single product is the only way to ensure quality.","That a good, well-defined process, if followed rigorously, will inherently lead to quality products.","That statistical methods are not useful for monitoring product quality.","That defects should be identified and eliminated only after the product is fully manufactured."],"ans":[1],"exp":"QA premise: if an organisation's processes are good and followed rigorously, products are bound to be good.","topic":"t-quality","lec":51},{"id":"AI-4-1","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":1,"n":1,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"According to the lecture on project management standards, what does PMBOK stand for?","opts":["Project Management Baseline of Knowledge","Process Management Body of Knowledge","Project Management Body of Knowledge","Program Management Baseline of Knowledge"],"ans":[2],"exp":"PMBOK = Project Management Body of Knowledge (PMI, USA), knowledge-based and non-prescriptive; PRINCE2 (UK) is process-based.","topic":"t-pm","lec":4},{"id":"AI-4-2","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":2,"n":2,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"In Scrum, what is the term for a fixed-duration iteration, typically one month long, during which a potentially shippable product increment is developed?","opts":["Cycle","Sprint","Phase","Increment"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Sprint: the Scrum iteration, target duration one month.","topic":"t-agile","lec":10},{"id":"AI-4-3","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":6,"n":3,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What is the primary purpose of a Work Breakdown Structure (WBS) in project scheduling?","opts":["To calculate the project's total cost","To assign resources to team members","To create a marketing plan for the product","To hierarchically decompose project work into smaller, manageable components"],"ans":[3],"exp":"WBS: hierarchical decomposition of work into manageable tasks; shows neither durations nor precedence.","topic":"t-wbs","lec":26},{"id":"AI-4-4","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":3,"n":4,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"In cost-benefit analysis, what does ROI stand for?","opts":["Rate of Interest","Return on Investment","Risk of Investment","Revenue of Initiative"],"ans":[1],"exp":"ROI = average annual profit / total investment × 100.","topic":"t-cba","lec":13},{"id":"AI-4-5","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":7,"n":5,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What is the primary characteristic of a 'Functional Organization' structure?","opts":["Teams are formed for a single project and then disbanded","Employees report to multiple project managers simultaneously","Employees are grouped by specialized functions like design or testing","All decisions are made by consensus among team members"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Functional organisation: grouped by specialisation (analysts, designers, testers); pyramidal; common in military, police, hospitals.","topic":"t-org","lec":47},{"id":"AI-4-6","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":9,"n":6,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What is the main goal of the 'Cleanroom Technique' in software development?","opts":["To test software in a sterile hardware environment","To prevent defects from entering the code in the first place","To write code that is easy for new developers to clean up","To ensure all developers have a clean and organized workspace"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Cleanroom: avoid defects by walkthroughs, inspection, formal verification; no execution-based testing by programmers.","topic":"t-codereview","lec":41},{"id":"AI-4-7","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":2,"n":7,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What is the primary purpose of a feasibility study in the software development lifecycle?","opts":["To write the initial code for the project","To determine if a project is viable before committing significant resources","To train the new development team","To create the final user manual"],"ans":[1],"exp":"Feasibility: decide viability before committing major resources.","topic":"t-waterfall","lec":5},{"id":"AI-4-8","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":4,"n":8,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"According to the lecture, what is the most significant drawback of the 'Price to Win' estimation method?","opts":["It is too time-consuming to calculate","It requires extensive historical data","The estimated costs do not accurately reflect the actual work required","It can only be used for small projects"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Price to win: you win the contract but costs do not reflect the work; either the customer gets less or overpays.","topic":"t-esttech","lec":17},{"id":"AI-4-9","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":10,"n":9,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What is the primary purpose of a Project Closure Report?","opts":["To request additional funding for the project","To assign new tasks to the project team","To document project outcomes, performance, and lessons learned for future projects","To market the newly completed software product"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Closure report: historical summary, survey results, performance data, final review findings, lessons learned and recommendations.","topic":"t-closeout","lec":50},{"id":"AI-4-10","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":6,"n":10,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"In PERT/CPM, what is the sequence of activities with zero slack that determines the minimum project duration called?","opts":["The Subcritical Path","The Optimal Route","The Critical Path","The Baseline Schedule"],"ans":[2],"exp":"Critical path: zero-float path that fixes the minimum project duration.","topic":"t-cpm","lec":28},{"id":"AI-4-11","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":6,"n":11,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"In a PERT/CPM forward pass, how is the Earliest Start (ES) time determined for an activity with multiple predecessors?","opts":["It is the earliest (minimum) of the Earliest Finish (EF) times of all its predecessors.","It is the average of the Earliest Finish (EF) times of all its predecessors.","It is the latest (maximum) of the Earliest Finish (EF) times of all its immediate predecessors.","It is determined by the project manager's discretion."],"ans":[2],"exp":"ES at a merge = max of predecessor EFs.","topic":"t-cpm","lec":29},{"id":"AI-4-12","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":7,"n":12,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"According to Herzberg's Two-Factor Theory, which of the following is considered a 'Motivator' rather than a 'Hygiene Factor'?","opts":["Company policy","Pay","Working conditions","Recognition of effort and performance"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Recognition is a motivator (satisfier).","topic":"t-people","lec":32},{"id":"AI-4-13","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":2,"n":13,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is the key difference between the RAD model and the traditional prototyping model?","opts":["The RAD model does not involve customer feedback.","In RAD, the prototype is refined into the final product, whereas in the prototyping model, it is typically thrown away.","The RAD model is only suitable for large, multi-year projects.","The prototyping model uses a 'time box' approach, while RAD does not."],"ans":[1],"exp":"RAD refines the prototype into the product; prototyping throws it away.","topic":"t-proto","lec":7},{"id":"AI-4-14","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":9,"n":14,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"In Earned Value Analysis, if a project has a Schedule Performance Index (SPI) of 0.88 and a Cost Performance Index (CPI) of 0.64, what does this indicate?","opts":["The project is ahead of schedule and under budget.","The project is ahead of schedule but over budget.","The project is behind schedule and over budget.","The project is behind schedule but under budget."],"ans":[2],"exp":"SPI 0.88 &lt; 1 behind schedule; CPI 0.64 &lt; 1 over budget.","topic":"t-eva","lec":44},{"id":"AI-4-15","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":12,"n":15,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is the primary reason software reliability measurement is more complex than hardware reliability measurement?","opts":["Software is more expensive than hardware.","Hardware failures are always caused by user error.","Software failures are due to design flaws, not physical wear, and reliability changes with each bug fix.","There are no standardized metrics for hardware reliability."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Software failures stem from design faults; reliability changes after each fix and depends on the user's operational profile.","topic":"t-reliab","lec":57},{"id":"AI-4-16","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":7,"n":16,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"How does a 'Project Organization' structure differ from a 'Functional Organization'?","opts":["A Project Organization is permanent, while a Functional Organization is temporary.","In a Project Organization, employees report to a functional manager, while in a Functional Organization, they report to a project manager.","A Project Organization offers less job variety for team members.","In a Project Organization, employees are dedicated to a single project team for its duration, whereas in a Functional Organization, they are drawn from specialized departments."],"ans":[3],"exp":"Project organisation: team dedicated to one project for its whole duration under the PM. Functional: people stay in specialist departments.","topic":"t-org","lec":47},{"id":"AI-4-17","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":10,"n":17,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"In the context of contract management, what is the main purpose of a Memoranda of Agreement (MoA)?","opts":["To serve as the final legal contract for payment.","To document the minutes of all project meetings.","To freeze the technical specifications of a solution so subsequent tenders can be judged primarily on price.","To outline the marketing strategy for the new product."],"ans":[2],"exp":"MoA: technical proposals are discussed and the agreed solution frozen; tenders are then judged on price.","topic":"t-contract","lec":49},{"id":"AI-4-18","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":2,"n":18,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"According to the lecture, what is a primary limitation of the classical waterfall model that the iterative waterfall model attempts to address?","opts":["Its lack of clear phases.","Its inability to handle rework and defects discovered in later phases.","Its unsuitability for projects with stable requirements.","Its over-reliance on customer feedback."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Classical waterfall assumes no defects; iterative waterfall adds feedback paths to rework earlier phases.","topic":"t-waterfall","lec":6},{"id":"AI-4-19","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":9,"n":19,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is the key difference between a 'slip chart' and a 'Gantt chart' for project monitoring?","opts":["A Gantt chart is used for cost monitoring, while a slip chart is for schedule monitoring.","A slip chart visually emphasizes deviations from the schedule, while a Gantt chart provides a static snapshot of planned durations.","Only Gantt charts can show the critical path.","Slip charts are used for initial planning, while Gantt charts are for post-implementation review."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Slip chart: the bend of the slip line shows deviation from plan; a Gantt chart is a static snapshot with a today cursor.","topic":"t-visual","lec":42},{"id":"AI-4-20","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":7,"n":20,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"In the context of risk management, what does 'Risk Reduction Leverage (RRL)' measure?","opts":["The total number of risks in a project.","The probability of a risk occurring.","The cost-effectiveness of a risk handling technique.","The amount of time a project is delayed by a risk."],"ans":[2],"exp":"RRL = (RE before − RE after) / cost of risk reduction; &gt; 1 means the measure is worth it.","topic":"t-risk","lec":35},{"id":"AI-4-21","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":4,"n":21,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is the primary purpose of the 'Value Adjustment Factor (VAF)' in Albrecht/IFPUG function point analysis?","opts":["To convert function points into lines of code.","To calculate the initial raw count of functional units.","To determine the number of user inputs and outputs.","To refine the raw functional size by accounting for technical and environmental complexity."],"ans":[3],"exp":"VAF refines the unadjusted count for technical and environmental complexity (14 GSCs).","topic":"t-fp","lec":19},{"id":"AI-4-22","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":11,"n":22,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"In the SEI CMM, what distinguishes Level 4 (Managed) from Level 3 (Defined)?","opts":["Level 4 introduces basic project management, while Level 3 is chaotic.","Level 4 focuses on continuous process improvement, while Level 3 focuses on repeating success.","Level 4 introduces quantitative measurement and control of processes, while Level 3 focuses on standardizing and documenting them.","Level 4 requires peer reviews, while Level 3 does not."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Level 4 (Managed): quantitative quality goals, process and product measured and controlled. Level 3: defined/documented process.","topic":"t-cmm","lec":55},{"id":"AI-4-23","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":5,"n":23,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"According to Putnam's model, if a project's development time is compressed by 50% (e.g., from 2 years to 1 year), by what factor does the required effort increase?","opts":["2 times","4 times","8 times","16 times"],"ans":[3],"exp":"Putnam: (2/1)⁴ = 16.","topic":"t-staffing","lec":25},{"id":"AI-4-24","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":6,"n":24,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"In a PERT/CPM backward pass, how is the Latest Finish (LF) time determined for an activity that is a 'burst point' (i.e., has multiple successors)?","opts":["It is the latest (maximum) of the Latest Start (LS) times of all its successors.","It is the smallest (minimum) of the Latest Start (LS) times of all its immediate successors.","It is the average of the Latest Start (LS) times of all its successors.","It is equal to the project's overall earliest finish time."],"ans":[1],"exp":"LF at a burst point = min of successors' LS.","topic":"t-cpm","lec":29},{"id":"AI-4-25","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":5,"n":25,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"In Halstead's Software Science, how is Programming Effort (E) calculated, and what does it represent?","opts":["E = V * L, representing the total lines of code.","E = N * log2(η), representing the program volume.","E = V^2 / V*, representing the number of mental discriminations required to implement the program.","E = V / S, representing the total development time in months."],"ans":[2],"exp":"E = V/L and L = V*/V, so E = V²/V*; E is the number of mental discriminations. Time T = E/S, S = 18.","topic":"t-halstead","lec":24},{"id":"AI-4-26","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":12,"n":26,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"What is a fundamental difference in how the Jelinski Moranda model and the Littlewood and Verall’s model account for reliability growth?","opts":["Jelinski Moranda is a step function model, while Littlewood and Verall's is linear.","Littlewood and Verall’s model accounts for negative reliability growth and diminishing returns, while Jelinski Moranda assumes reliability improvement is proportional to remaining faults.","Jelinski Moranda is used for hardware reliability, while Littlewood and Verall's is for software.","Littlewood and Verall’s model assumes each bug fix contributes equally to reliability, which Jelinski Moranda refutes."],"ans":[1],"exp":"J-M: failure rate ∝ remaining faults. L-V: each fix's contribution is a random variable (gamma), allows negative growth and diminishing returns.","topic":"t-relgrowth","lec":59},{"id":"AI-4-27","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":9,"n":27,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"A project manager is using the 0/100 technique for earned value. A task with a Planned Value (PV) of $10,000 is scheduled for completion by week 4. At the end of week 4, the task is 90% complete with an Actual Cost (AC) of $8,000. What are the Earned Value (EV), Cost Variance (CV), and Schedule Variance (SV)?","opts":["EV = $9,000, CV = $1,000, SV = -$1,000","EV = $0, CV = -$8,000, SV = -$10,000","EV = $10,000, CV = $2,000, SV = $0","EV = $0, CV = $2,000, SV = -$10,000"],"ans":[1],"exp":"0/100: no value until 100 % done, so EV = 0. CV = EV − AC = −8,000; SV = EV − PV = −10,000.","topic":"t-eva","lec":43},{"id":"AI-4-28","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":11,"n":28,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"Why is an organization with ISO 9001 certification prohibited from advertising its individual products as 'ISO 9000 compliant'?","opts":["Because ISO 9001 certification is only valid for one year.","Because the ISO organization charges a separate fee for product-level advertising.","Because ISO 9001 certifies the organization's quality process, not the individual products themselves.","Because only organizations in the manufacturing sector can advertise product compliance."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Certification is of the organisation's process, not products.","topic":"t-iso","lec":52},{"id":"AI-4-29","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":7,"n":29,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"In the context of team structures, why is a hierarchical team structure considered more suitable for large projects than a democratic team structure?","opts":["A hierarchical team is less expensive to manage.","A hierarchical structure scales effectively by distributing leadership, whereas the N-squared communication paths in a democratic team become highly inefficient as team size increases.","Democratic teams are only suitable for maintenance projects.","Hierarchical teams do not require a project manager."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Communication paths grow as n(n−1)/2 in a democratic team; a hierarchy limits paths and shares leadership with senior programmers.","topic":"t-org","lec":34},{"id":"AI-4-30","src":"AI","paper":4,"wk":5,"n":30,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"In COCOMO II's Application Composition Model, what three components are counted to determine the Number of Application Points (NAP)?","opts":["Number of lines of code, number of developers, and project duration.","Number of use cases, number of actors, and number of classes.","Number of separate screens, number of reports produced, and number of modules.","Number of hardware devices, number of databases, and number of network connections."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Application (object) points: screens (1/2/3), reports (2/5/8) and 3GL modules (10 each).","topic":"t-cocomo2","lec":23},{"id":"AI-5-1","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":6,"n":1,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"According to the lecture, what is the primary purpose of a Work Breakdown Structure (WBS)?","opts":["To calculate the project's total cost and budget.","To create a hierarchical decomposition of project work into smaller, manageable components.","To assign specific developers to each task in the project.","To determine the critical path of the project."],"ans":[1],"exp":"WBS: hierarchical decomposition of work into manageable components.","topic":"t-wbs","lec":26},{"id":"AI-5-2","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":2,"n":2,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"In the Scrum framework, what is a 'Sprint'?","opts":["A long-term project plan spanning several months.","A daily 15-minute meeting for the development team.","A fixed-duration iteration, typically one month long, to develop a product increment.","The final phase of the project where all features are integrated."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Sprint: fixed-duration (target one month) iteration producing an increment.","topic":"t-agile","lec":10},{"id":"AI-5-3","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":4,"n":3,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What does Parkinson's Law state in the context of project estimation?","opts":["The best way to estimate a project is to break it down into its smallest parts.","Work expands to fill the time available.","A project without a reliability requirement can meet any other requirement.","The more people you add to a late project, the later it gets."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Parkinson's law: work expands to fill the time available (over-estimation risk).","topic":"t-estbasics","lec":16},{"id":"AI-5-4","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":8,"n":4,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"In project monitoring and control, what is a 'milestone'?","opts":["A daily task assigned to a junior developer.","A designated key event or significant point in a project's timeline.","The total budget allocated for a project phase.","An informal discussion about project progress."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Milestone: a key event such as completion of the SRS review; reaching it shows measurable progress.","topic":"t-monitor","lec":39},{"id":"AI-5-5","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":9,"n":5,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"In Earned Value Analysis, what is 'Planned Value (PV)'?","opts":["The actual cost incurred for the work performed.","The total value credited to a project at a specific point in time.","The original budgeted cost for work scheduled to be completed by a given date.","The final profit earned at the end of the project."],"ans":[2],"exp":"PV (BCWS): budgeted cost of the work scheduled to be done by a given date.","topic":"t-eva","lec":43},{"id":"AI-5-6","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":7,"n":6,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What is the Hawthorne Effect?","opts":["The tendency for project scope to expand over time.","The phenomenon where individuals improve performance in response to being observed.","The principle that 90% of execution time is spent in 10% of the code.","A management style that assumes employees are inherently lazy."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Hawthorne experiments: productivity rose whatever the lighting because the workers were being observed (interest shown in them).","topic":"t-people","lec":31},{"id":"AI-5-7","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":7,"n":7,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What is a 'functional organization' structure?","opts":["A structure where teams are formed for a single project and then disbanded.","A structure where employees are grouped by specialized functions like design or testing.","A hybrid structure with dual reporting lines to project and functional managers.","A flat structure where all decisions are made by consensus."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Functional organisation: grouped by function/specialisation.","topic":"t-org","lec":33},{"id":"AI-5-8","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":8,"n":8,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What is a 'resource histogram' used for in project management?","opts":["To calculate the total cost of all project resources.","To list all activities on the critical path.","To visualize the demand for a specific resource over the project's timeline.","To document the project's communication plan."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Resource histogram: demand for a resource over time.","topic":"t-resource","lec":37},{"id":"AI-5-9","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":10,"n":9,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"In contract management, what is a 'bespoke system'?","opts":["A generalized software package available for immediate purchase.","A software system specifically created and tailored for a single customer's unique requirements.","A core system that is purchased and then customized to meet specific needs.","A contract where the customer is charged per hour of work."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Bespoke system: created specially for one customer.","topic":"t-contract","lec":18},{"id":"AI-5-10","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":1,"n":10,"type":"mcq","diff":"Easy","text":"What is meant by the 'intangibility' of software?","opts":["Software is very easy to change and modify.","Software does not consume physical space or have weight.","Software progress is difficult to visually assess until it is fully operational.","Software development is primarily intellectual work."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Intangible: software is invisible until it is ready and runs, so progress is hard to see.","topic":"t-intro","lec":1},{"id":"AI-5-11","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":2,"n":11,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"In the Incremental Model, how is the Value-to-Cost (V/C) ratio used?","opts":["To calculate the total net profit of the project.","To determine the number of developers needed for a task.","To prioritize which increments to develop next by comparing customer value to development cost.","To decide whether to use the Waterfall model or the RAD model."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Value-to-cost: score value 1-10 (customer) and cost 0-10 (developers); develop the highest V/C increment first.","topic":"t-proto","lec":7},{"id":"AI-5-12","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":2,"n":12,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is the primary role of the Scrum Master in a Scrum team?","opts":["To define features and prioritize the Product Backlog.","To write the code for the most complex user stories.","To act as a servant-leader who facilitates the process and removes impediments.","To represent the customer's interests and accept or reject completed work."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Scrum master: essentially the project manager; removes impediments, shields the team, facilitates the process.","topic":"t-agile","lec":10},{"id":"AI-5-13","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":3,"n":13,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is the key difference between Project Risk and Business Risk?","opts":["Project Risk relates to budget overruns, while Business Risk relates to schedule delays.","Project Risk threatens the successful execution of the project, while Business Risk threatens the benefits of the delivered project.","Project Risk is managed by the development team, while Business Risk is managed by the customer.","Project Risk is only relevant for technical projects, while Business Risk applies to all projects."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Project risk vs business risk (see business case).","topic":"t-bizcase","lec":11},{"id":"AI-5-14","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":3,"n":14,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"How does Net Present Value (NPV) analysis improve upon evaluation techniques like ROI and Payback Period?","opts":["It is simpler to calculate and requires less data.","It provides a result as a percentage, which is easier to understand.","It ignores future cash flows, focusing only on the initial investment.","It accounts for the time value of money by discounting future cash flows."],"ans":[3],"exp":"NPV considers both profitability and the timing of cash flows.","topic":"t-cba","lec":13},{"id":"AI-5-15","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":6,"n":15,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"In a PERT/CPM network, what is the rule for calculating the Earliest Start (ES) time for an activity at a merge point (an activity with multiple predecessors)?","opts":["It is the average of the Earliest Finish (EF) times of all its predecessors.","It is the earliest (minimum) of the Earliest Finish (EF) times of all its predecessors.","It is the latest (maximum) of the Earliest Finish (EF) times of all its predecessors.","It is determined by the duration of the longest predecessor activity."],"ans":[2],"exp":"ES at a merge = max EF of predecessors.","topic":"t-cpm","lec":29},{"id":"AI-5-16","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":2,"n":16,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"How does the V-Model emphasize verification and validation compared to the classical waterfall model?","opts":["It removes the testing phase entirely to speed up development.","It integrates test planning and design activities into every development phase.","It performs all testing only after the coding phase is 100% complete.","It relies on customer feedback instead of formal testing activities."],"ans":[1],"exp":"V-model plans testing in parallel with each development phase.","topic":"t-waterfall","lec":6},{"id":"AI-5-17","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":2,"n":17,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is the purpose of 'refactoring' in agile development?","opts":["To add new features requested by the customer during an iteration.","To fix bugs discovered during the testing phase.","To restructure existing code without changing its external behavior, to improve its internal design.","To create the initial design metaphor for the project."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Refactoring: restructure code without changing external behaviour.","topic":"t-agile","lec":9},{"id":"AI-5-18","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":4,"n":18,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"In the IFPUG function point method, how is the Value Adjustment Factor (VAF) calculated?","opts":["By multiplying the number of user inputs by the number of user outputs.","By summing the weights of all functional units.","By using the formula VAF = 0.65 + (0.01 * TDI), where TDI is the Total Degree of Influence.","By dividing the estimated lines of code by the number of function points."],"ans":[2],"exp":"VAF = 0.65 + 0.01 × TDI.","topic":"t-fp","lec":19},{"id":"AI-5-19","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":6,"n":19,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is a subcritical path and why is it important for project managers to monitor?","opts":["It is a path with negative float, indicating the project is already late.","It is the shortest path in the network, representing the fastest possible completion.","It is a path where activities have a very small, but non-zero, amount of float, which could easily become critical.","It is a path that has been intentionally delayed to save costs."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Sub-critical paths have small float; a slip can make them critical, so monitor them too.","topic":"t-cpm","lec":30},{"id":"AI-5-20","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":7,"n":20,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is a primary advantage of a project organization structure in terms of job satisfaction?","opts":["It allows specialists to focus on a single type of task, enhancing their expertise.","It provides job variety and rotation, as team members engage in diverse activities.","It guarantees lifetime employment on a single project.","It minimizes communication, allowing developers to work in isolation."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Project organisation advantages: job rotation/variety, easy communication, easier staffing.","topic":"t-org","lec":33},{"id":"AI-5-21","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":10,"n":21,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"What is the primary advantage of a Time and Material contract for the customer?","opts":["The total project cost is fixed and known upfront.","The supplier has a strong incentive to be cost-effective.","It is easy to change or add new requirements during development.","It shifts all financial risk to the supplier."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Time &amp; materials: easy to change requirements, no price pressure on quality; but the customer carries the risk.","topic":"t-contract","lec":18},{"id":"AI-5-22","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":4,"n":22,"type":"mcq","diff":"Medium","text":"In the context of algorithmic estimation, what is the purpose of 'model calibration'?","opts":["To select the programming language for the project.","To create a detailed Work Breakdown Structure (WBS).","To customize a generic model to fit specific organizational needs and project characteristics.","To calculate the final price charged to the customer."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Calibration customises a generic model (COCOMO etc.) to the organisation's environment using past project data.","topic":"t-esttech","lec":18},{"id":"AI-5-23","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":10,"n":23,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"In the contract tendering process, what is the primary purpose of establishing a Memoranda of Agreement (MoA)?","opts":["To finalize the payment schedule and contract price with the chosen vendor.","To invite as many suppliers as possible to bid on the project.","To freeze the technical specifications so that subsequent tenders can be judged primarily on price.","To legally bind all potential bidders to a non-disclosure agreement."],"ans":[2],"exp":"MoA freezes the technical solution so tenders compete on price.","topic":"t-contract","lec":49},{"id":"AI-5-24","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":4,"n":24,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"Why is Weinberg's Zeroth Law of Reliability ('a software project that does not have to meet a reliability requirement can meet any other requirement') a critical principle in project estimation?","opts":["It implies that reliability is the easiest requirement to achieve.","It suggests that if reliability is not a strict requirement, other aspects might be prioritized at its expense, affecting true project cost and quality.","It proves that over-estimating project duration always leads to better reliability.","It states that reliability can only be estimated after the project is 100% complete."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Weinberg's zeroth law: a project that does not have to meet a reliability requirement can meet any other requirement (quality is what gets squeezed).","topic":"t-estbasics","lec":16},{"id":"AI-5-25","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":9,"n":25,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"What is the defining characteristic of the Cleanroom technique for bug removal?","opts":["It relies exclusively on automated testing tools to find all defects.","It requires managers to personally review and approve every line of code.","Programmers are strictly not allowed to test their code by executing it for bug removal.","It is an informal process where developers mentally simulate code execution."],"ans":[2],"exp":"Cleanroom: no execution-based testing by programmers; walkthroughs, inspection and formal verification instead.","topic":"t-codereview","lec":41},{"id":"AI-5-26","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":12,"n":26,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"Why is Probability of Failure On Demand (POFOD) often a more relevant reliability metric for software systems than time-based metrics like MTTF?","opts":["POFOD is easier to calculate without any user data.","Software often operates on demand rather than continuously, making the success rate of individual invocations more meaningful.","POFOD accounts for the physical wear and tear of software components.","POFOD is the only metric that can be used for hardware reliability."],"ans":[1],"exp":"POFOD measures failure probability per service request; it does not involve time, so it suits on-demand systems.","topic":"t-reliab","lec":58},{"id":"AI-5-27","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":2,"n":27,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"Why is the Spiral Model considered a 'meta-model'?","opts":["It is the oldest and most foundational of all lifecycle models.","It can only be applied to metaphysical or abstract software problems.","It is exclusively focused on risk handling and ignores other development activities.","It can incorporate aspects of waterfall, iterative, and prototyping models within its loops."],"ans":[3],"exp":"Spiral subsumes waterfall, prototyping and evolutionary development: meta-model.","topic":"t-proto","lec":8},{"id":"AI-5-28","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":7,"n":28,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"How is Risk Reduction Leverage (RRL) used to make decisions about which risk handling techniques to implement?","opts":["A higher RRL indicates a risk is more severe and should be avoided at all costs.","An RRL greater than 1 indicates the technique is cost-effective because the reduction in risk exposure outweighs the cost.","RRL is calculated by dividing the cost of the technique by the potential damage.","An RRL less than 1 means the risk handling technique is guaranteed to eliminate the risk."],"ans":[1],"exp":"RRL &gt; 1 means the reduction in risk exposure is bigger than the cost of the measure: worth doing.","topic":"t-risk","lec":35},{"id":"AI-5-29","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":10,"n":29,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"What is the key difference between a 'version' and a 'revision' in the context of Software Configuration Management (SCM)?","opts":["A version refers to source code, while a revision refers to documentation.","A version is a specific configuration of the entire product at a point in time, while a revision is a successive state of a single configuration item.","A version is created by the project manager, while a revision is created by a junior developer.","Versions are used for internal tracking, while revisions are released to customers."],"ans":[1],"exp":"Version: configuration (set of items) at a point in time. Revision: successive state of one configuration item.","topic":"t-scm","lec":47},{"id":"AI-5-30","src":"AI","paper":5,"wk":5,"n":30,"type":"mcq","diff":"Hard","text":"According to Putnam's model, what is the expected impact on total effort if a project's development time is compressed by 50%?","opts":["The effort will decrease by 50%.","The effort will double (increase by 2 times).","The effort will quadruple (increase by 4 times).","The effort will increase by 16 times."],"ans":[3],"exp":"Putnam: halving td → 16× effort.","topic":"t-staffing","lec":25}];
